var e={"tech:pointcontact":{intro:`The point-contact transistor is a tiny solid amplifier: two gold contacts pressed very close together onto a slab of germanium crystal, with a third connection on the base underneath. A weak signal fed into one contact controls a much stronger current flowing out of the other. It does the job of a vacuum tube with no hot filament, no glass bulb and almost no power.`,how:`Germanium is a semiconductor: it conducts a little, and its behaviour changes when extra charge is pushed into it. The contacts are made by wrapping gold foil round a plastic wedge and slitting it at the tip with a razor, so the two edges sit about the width of a hair apart. Current injected at one contact, the emitter, spreads into the crystal and lets far more flow to the other, the collector. The device is fragile and noisy, and no two behave quite alike.`,history:`John Bardeen and Walter Brattain, working in William Shockley's solid-state group at Bell Labs in New Jersey, first made a point-contact transistor amplify on 16 December 1947. On 23 December 1947 they showed it to Bell Labs managers by using it to amplify speech. Bell Labs announced the invention to the press on 30 June 1948, with the name 'transistor', short for transfer resistor. Western Electric began manufacturing point-contact transistors in 1951, though they varied widely from one to the next. Bardeen, Brattain and Shockley shared the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1956.`,facts:[`The two gold contacts of the first transistor were about 50 micrometres apart (1947).`,`From 1952 Bell Labs licensed its transistor patents to other firms for $25,000 each.`,`Shockley, Bardeen and Brattain shared the 1956 Nobel Prize in Physics.`]},"tech:puresi":{intro:`A transistor only works if its crystal is almost perfect and almost perfectly clean: even a few foreign atoms in every hundred million change how it conducts. Ultrapure crystals are made in two steps. First a single unbroken crystal is grown from molten germanium or silicon; then the impurities are swept out of it with a slowly moving band of heat.`,how:`In crystal pulling, a small seed crystal is dipped into the melt and drawn upwards very slowly while turning, so atoms freeze onto it in one continuous lattice instead of many jumbled grains. In zone refining, a narrow molten band is passed along a bar again and again. Most impurities prefer to stay in the liquid, so each pass carries them towards one end, which is cut off and thrown away.`,history:`Gordon Teal and John Little at Bell Labs were growing large single crystals of germanium by pulling them from the melt by 1950, adapting a method Jan Czochralski had found in Poland in 1916. William Pfann at Bell Labs invented zone refining in 1951. Pfann published the method in 1952, and germanium refined this way became the purest material ever made in quantity. Silicon was harder, because it melts at about 1,410 degrees Celsius and attacks its container; by 1956 Bell Labs had a floating-zone method in which the molten band hangs between two solid ends with no crucible at all.`,facts:[`Czochralski found crystal pulling in 1916 when he dipped his pen into molten tin instead of ink.`,`Zone refining could cut impurities to about one atom in ten billion (1952).`]},"tech:junction":{intro:`The junction transistor is a sandwich inside a single crystal: a very thin middle layer of one type of semiconductor between two thicker layers of the other type. With no metal points pressing on the surface, it is sturdier, quieter and more predictable than the point-contact transistor, and it runs on tiny amounts of power.`,how:`Adding traces of certain elements makes germanium n-type, carrying current with spare electrons, or p-type, carrying it with 'holes' where electrons are missing. In an n-p-n sandwich, a small current into the thin p-type base lets a much larger current cross from the emitter to the collector. The thinner the base, the faster the transistor. Early ones were grown by changing the doping of the melt while the crystal was being pulled.`,history:`William Shockley at Bell Labs worked out the theory of the junction transistor in January 1948, weeks after Bardeen and Brattain's point-contact success. Morgan Sparks and Gordon Teal grew working n-p-n junctions inside a single pulled crystal, and Bell Labs announced the junction transistor in July 1951. In 1952 General Electric and RCA showed alloy-junction transistors, made by melting dots of indium into both faces of a thin germanium wafer. The Sonotone 1010 hearing aid of 1952 was the first consumer product to use a transistor. The Regency TR-1 of 1954, the first transistor radio sold, used four Texas Instruments junction transistors.`,facts:[`Raytheon's CK722 junction transistor sold to hobbyists for about $7.60 in 1953.`,`The Regency TR-1 radio of 1954 cost $49.95 and ran on a 22.5-volt battery.`]},"tech:siliconxtor":{intro:`A silicon transistor does the same job as a germanium one but is made from silicon, the element in sand. It is harder to purify and needs much higher furnace temperatures, but it keeps working when hot, where germanium transistors falter above about 75 degrees Celsius. That makes it the transistor that missiles, aircraft and computers want.`,how:`Silicon holds on to its electrons more tightly than germanium, so heat shakes far fewer of them loose and the unwanted leakage current stays small. Silicon also melts at over 1,400 degrees Celsius and reacts with almost every crucible, which is why it took longer to master. Its oxide, a hard glass, can be grown on the surface to protect it, a gift germanium does not have.`,history:`Morris Tanenbaum at Bell Labs made the first silicon transistor on 26 January 1954, but Bell Labs did not announce it. Gordon Teal, who had moved from Bell Labs to Texas Instruments in Dallas, revealed silicon transistors in production at a conference in Dayton, Ohio, on 10 May 1954. In that 1954 talk he played music through a germanium transistor dipped in hot oil until it fell silent, then swapped in a silicon one, which played on. For several years from 1954 Texas Instruments was the leading maker of silicon transistors, selling mostly to the US military. By the late 1960s silicon transistors outsold germanium ones.`,facts:[`Teal announced Texas Instruments' silicon transistors on 10 May 1954 in Dayton, Ohio.`,`Silicon melts at about 1,414 °C, germanium at about 938 °C (known well before 1954).`]},"tech:diffusedxtor":{intro:`Diffusion is a way of doping a semiconductor by baking it: the slices sit in a hot furnace filled with a gas of impurity atoms, which soak slowly into the surface. Because the depth depends on time and temperature, layers less than a micrometre thick can be made reliably. That gives transistors very thin bases, which switch far faster than grown or alloyed ones.`,how:`At around a thousand degrees Celsius, atoms of boron or phosphorus wander into the silicon crystal, a little like ink spreading into blotting paper. Baking four times as long takes them only twice as deep, so depth can be tuned finely. Two diffusions, one of each type, make the base and emitter of a transistor. Many slices go through one furnace together, which suits mass production.`,history:`Calvin Fuller at Bell Labs developed gas diffusion of impurities into silicon and germanium in 1954, and used it that year in the first practical silicon solar cell. In 1955 Charles Lee at Bell Labs made a diffused-base germanium transistor and Morris Tanenbaum a diffused silicon one, both far faster than anything grown or alloyed. In 1956 Bell Labs taught diffusion methods to the companies licensing its patents, and the technique spread through the industry. Fairchild Semiconductor, founded in 1957, built its whole business on diffused silicon transistors.`,facts:[`The diffused Bell Labs solar cell of 1954 turned about 6% of sunlight into electricity.`,`From 1954 makers set doping depth by bake time: four times the bake, twice the depth.`]},"tech:photolitho":{intro:`Photolithography prints patterns onto a semiconductor slice with light, much as a photograph is printed from a negative. A thin coat of light-sensitive lacquer, called photoresist, is exposed through a mask and developed, leaving bare windows where acid can etch the layer beneath. One mask prints the same pattern over the whole slice at once, so dozens of devices are made together.`,how:`First the silicon is heated in steam or oxygen to grow a skin of silicon dioxide, a glass that stops dopant atoms getting through. Resist is spun on, the mask is pressed flat against it and ultraviolet light shines through. After developing, hydrofluoric acid dissolves the oxide only where the resist has gone, and in the diffusion furnace dopant enters only through those windows. Repeating this with several masks, carefully lined up, builds a transistor layer by layer.`,history:`Carl Frosch and Lincoln Derick at Bell Labs found in 1955 that a layer of silicon dioxide grown on silicon could block dopants and so act as a stencil for diffusion. Jay Lathrop and James Nall at the US Army's Diamond Ordnance Fuze Laboratories used photoresist and a microscope run backwards to shrink patterns, coined the word photolithography, and filed a patent in 1957. Frosch and Derick published their oxide-masking results in 1957, and Fairchild Semiconductor adopted both ideas when it was founded that year. In 1958 Lathrop moved to Texas Instruments to set up its photolithography.`,facts:[`Frosch and Derick's oxide first grew by accident in 1955, when hydrogen flared and filled their furnace with steam.`,`Early chipmakers of the late 1950s bought photoresist from Kodak, which made it for etching metal printing plates.`]},"tech:mesa":{intro:`A mesa transistor is a diffused silicon transistor whose surroundings have been etched away, leaving each device standing on the slice like a tiny flat-topped hill, named after the table mountains of the American desert. Removing the extra material cuts the stray capacitance that slows a transistor down, making it fast enough for computer circuits.`,how:`The whole slice is diffused to form the base layer, and small aluminium and gold contacts are evaporated on top through masks. Wax or resist protects the area of each transistor while acid eats the rest down below the junction, so the collector junction is only as wide as the mesa. The edges of the junction are left bare, however, so a speck of dirt or a trace of moisture can ruin the transistor unless it is sealed in a metal can.`,history:`Fairchild Semiconductor was founded in California in October 1957 by eight engineers, including Robert Noyce, Gordon Moore and Jean Hoerni, who had walked out of William Shockley's laboratory. Its first product, the 2N697 double-diffused silicon mesa transistor, went on sale in 1958. IBM bought the first 100 in 1958 for a computer aboard the planned B-70 bomber. In 1959 Fairchild found that loose specks inside the sealed cans could land on the bare junction edges and ruin a transistor, which pushed it towards a better design.`,facts:[`IBM paid $150 each for Fairchild's first 100 mesa transistors (1958).`,`Each of Fairchild's eight founders put in $500 of his own money (1957).`,`Fairchild Camera and Instrument provided about $1.38 million to start the company (1957).`]},"tech:planar":{intro:`The planar process builds transistors flat inside the silicon, under the glassy oxide used to mask them, instead of etching them into hills. The edges of every junction stay buried beneath the oxide, sealed from dirt and moisture from the moment they are made, and metal contacts reach them through small windows. The finished surface is flat enough to print wiring on.`,how:`Each diffusion is done through a window cut in the oxide, and the dopant spreads a little sideways under the window's edge, so the junction ends up covered by glass. Fresh oxide is grown over the window before the next step. At the end, holes are opened for the contacts and a thin film of aluminium is evaporated and patterned to join them. Because everything happens on one flat face, many masks can be lined up one after another.`,history:`Jean Hoerni, a Swiss-born physicist and one of Fairchild's founders, wrote down the planar idea in his notebook on 1 December 1957. Fairchild made working planar transistors early in 1959 and filed Hoerni's patent that year. In January 1959 Robert Noyce saw that the flat oxide could also carry metal wires joining several devices on one slice. Fairchild launched the planar 2N1613 transistor in 1960, and its reliability won over military customers. By the mid-1960s nearly every silicon transistor and chip in the world was made by the planar process, much of it under licence from Fairchild.`,facts:[`Hoerni's notebook entry describing the planar process is dated 1 December 1957.`,`Noyce's notebook entry joining planar devices with metal wiring is dated 23 January 1959.`]},"tech:monolithic":{intro:`An integrated circuit is a complete electronic circuit made in a single piece of semiconductor: transistors, resistors and capacitors formed side by side in one chip and joined by wiring on its surface. It replaces boards of separate parts soldered together by hand, so circuits become smaller, lighter and more reliable, and many can be made at a time.`,how:`In the planar version, all the parts are diffused into one silicon chip through windows in the oxide. A layer of aluminium is evaporated over the top and etched into wires, and the oxide keeps the wires from touching the silicon except at the contacts. Neighbouring parts are kept electrically apart by junctions that act like insulating walls. A slice holds many identical circuits, which are tested, cut apart and sealed in small packages.`,history:`Jack Kilby at Texas Instruments in Dallas demonstrated the first integrated circuit on 12 September 1958: a slip of germanium holding a transistor, resistors and a capacitor, joined by fine gold wires. Robert Noyce at Fairchild described a planar integrated circuit with its wiring printed on the oxide in January 1959, and filed a patent in July 1959. A Fairchild team under Jay Last made the first working planar integrated circuit in May 1960. Fairchild began selling its Micrologic chips in March 1961, and Texas Instruments launched its Series 51 in 1961 as well. In 1962 the US Air Force chose integrated circuits for the Minuteman II missile and NASA chose them for the Apollo guidance computer, making the government the biggest early buyer.`,facts:[`Kilby's first chip of 1958 measured about 11 by 1.6 millimetres.`,`The average integrated circuit sold for about $50 in 1962 and about $2.33 in 1968.`,`Jack Kilby received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2000.`]},"tech:epitaxy":{intro:`Epitaxy grows a thin new layer of crystal on top of a silicon slice, atom by atom, so that it carries on the slice's crystal pattern perfectly. The new layer can be doped quite differently from the slice beneath it. That lets a fast transistor be built in a thin, lightly doped film resting on a heavily doped, low-resistance base, something a single grown crystal cannot offer.`,how:`The slice is heated to over a thousand degrees Celsius in a reactor while a gas such as silicon tetrachloride, mixed with hydrogen, flows over it. The gas breaks down at the hot surface and silicon atoms settle into place following the crystal below, building a film a few micrometres thick in minutes. A little dopant gas in the mix sets the new layer's type. In a transistor the collector sits in the thin film, so current has only a short, easy path to the contact.`,history:`A Bell Labs team led by Ian Ross, including Henry Theuerer, announced the epitaxial silicon transistor in 1960. Its thin film cut the collector's resistance, so the 1960 device switched much faster than earlier diffused transistors. Fairchild, Texas Instruments and others took up epitaxy within a year or two, and by the mid-1960s almost every bipolar integrated circuit was built on an epitaxial layer. In 1963 Herbert Nelson at RCA developed liquid-phase epitaxy, which grew the layers of gallium arsenide lasers and light-emitting diodes.`,facts:[`The word epitaxy, Greek for 'arranged upon', was coined by the French mineralogist Louis Royer in 1928.`,`Bell's epitaxial layers of 1960 were a few micrometres thick, thinner than a red blood cell is wide.`]},"tech:mosfet":{intro:`The MOS transistor, short for metal-oxide-semiconductor, is a switch made of three layers: a metal plate, a thin film of glass and the silicon beneath. A voltage on the plate, called the gate, draws a thin channel of charge into the silicon, letting current flow between two contacts. Almost no current flows into the gate itself. It is slower than a junction transistor but simpler and smaller.`,how:`Two doped regions, the source and the drain, are diffused into the silicon a short distance apart, and the gate sits over the gap, insulated by oxide. With no voltage on the gate the two regions are cut off from each other; with enough voltage, charge gathers under the oxide and forms a conducting channel, like opening a tap. One MOS transistor needs a single diffusion and no isolating walls, so many more fit in an area, though early ones drifted as stray sodium atoms moved in the oxide.`,history:`Mohamed Atalla and Dawon Kahng at Bell Labs presented the first working silicon MOS transistor in 1960, building on Atalla's discovery that a grown oxide layer tames the silicon surface. Bell Labs saw little use for it in 1960, because it was far slower than bipolar transistors. Steven Hofstein and Frederic Heiman at RCA built an experimental MOS integrated circuit in 1962. General Microelectronics, founded by former Fairchild engineers, sold the first commercial MOS integrated circuits in 1964. In 1965 Fairchild researchers, among them Bruce Deal and Andrew Grove, traced the drifting of MOS transistors to sodium contamination, and cleaner furnaces began to cure it.`,facts:[`RCA's experimental MOS chip of 1962 held 16 transistors.`,`The first commercial MOS chips of 1964 came from General Microelectronics in California.`]},"tech:cleanroom":{intro:`A laminar-flow cleanroom keeps dust off delicate work by pushing filtered air through the room in one steady direction, from ceiling to floor or from one wall to the opposite one. Instead of being stirred round, every speck is carried straight out. A single particle landing on a chip can break a wire or short a transistor, so cleaner air means more chips that work.`,how:`Fans push air through high-efficiency filters covering the whole ceiling, and it leaves through grilles in the floor, replacing the air in the room several times a minute. Workers wear gowns, caps, masks and gloves, because people shed huge numbers of skin flakes and fibres. Rooms are graded by how many particles of a given size a cubic foot of air may hold: the fewer, the better the class.`,history:`Willis Whitfield, a physicist at Sandia Laboratory in Albuquerque, New Mexico, designed the laminar-flow cleanroom in 1960 to keep dust out of precision weapon parts. When Sandia described it in 1962, it was about a thousand times cleaner than the best rooms then in use. In 1963 the US government issued Federal Standard 209, which rated cleanrooms by the number of particles per cubic foot of air. Chip factories adopted laminar flow through the 1960s, and NASA used it to assemble spacecraft. By the late 1980s the best chip fabs were building Class 1 rooms, with no more than one particle of half a micrometre or larger in each cubic foot.`,facts:[`Whitfield's design of 1962 was about 1,000 times cleaner than earlier cleanrooms.`,`Federal Standard 209, first issued in 1963, graded rooms by particles per cubic foot of air.`]},"tech:cmos":{intro:`CMOS, complementary MOS, builds logic from pairs of MOS transistors of opposite types, one n-channel and one p-channel, wired so that whenever one is on, the other is off. A gate built this way draws current only during the instant it switches. When it is resting it uses almost no power at all, which makes it ideal for things that run on small batteries.`,how:`In the simplest gate, an inverter, the p-channel transistor connects the output to the supply and the n-channel one connects it to ground, and both gates share the input. Whichever state the input is in, one path is blocked, so no steady current flows. Making both types on one slice needs an extra 'well' of opposite doping for one of them, so CMOS takes more steps and more space than other MOS logic, and early versions are slow.`,history:`Frank Wanlass and Chih-Tang Sah at Fairchild presented CMOS logic at the International Solid-State Circuits Conference in February 1963. Wanlass filed a patent on the idea in June 1963. RCA launched the CD4000 series of CMOS logic chips in 1968, popular for battery-powered and low-noise equipment. Japanese makers such as Toshiba and Seiko built CMOS chips into calculators and quartz watches in the early 1970s. In 1978 Hitachi's Hi-CMOS static memory matched the speed of NMOS chips, a sign that CMOS was catching up.`,facts:[`Wanlass and Sah's 1963 paper was titled 'Nanowatt Logic', after the tiny power their gates drew at rest.`,`RCA's CD4000 chips of 1968 worked on any supply from about 3 to 15 volts.`]},"tech:silicongate":{intro:`Silicon-gate MOS replaces the aluminium gate of the MOS transistor with a strip of polycrystalline silicon, a grainy form of silicon that conducts when doped. The silicon gate is laid down before the source and drain are made and then acts as their stencil, so each gate lines up exactly with the regions on either side. Chips get faster, denser and more reliable.`,how:`With aluminium gates, the gate had to be printed after the source and drain and made extra wide to cover any misalignment, adding overlap that slowed the transistor. A silicon gate survives the heat of the diffusion furnace, so it goes on first and the dopant enters the silicon only on either side of it: the gate is self-aligned. The polysilicon also serves as an extra layer of wiring, and its lower threshold voltage lets MOS chips work with ordinary logic levels.`,history:`Federico Faggin and Tom Klein at Fairchild's research laboratory in Palo Alto developed a practical silicon-gate process in 1968, building on earlier self-aligned gate work at Bell Labs. Their first product, the Fairchild 3708, an eight-channel analogue switch, went on sale in 1968. Robert Noyce and Gordon Moore left Fairchild to found Intel in July 1968 and made silicon gate the basis of its memory chips. Intel's 1101 static memory of 1969 and its 1103 dynamic memory of 1970 both used silicon gates. Faggin joined Intel in 1970 and used the process to build the 4004 microprocessor in 1971.`,facts:[`The silicon-gate 3708 of 1968 was several times faster than the aluminium-gate chip it replaced.`,`Intel's 1101 of 1969 stored 256 bits on one silicon-gate chip.`]},"tech:ionimplant":{intro:`Ion implantation dopes silicon by shooting it with a beam of electrically charged atoms from a small particle accelerator. The number of atoms is set by measuring the beam's current and their depth by its energy, so doping can be controlled far more precisely than by baking in a furnace. It is the tool that sets exactly when a MOS transistor turns on.`,how:`Atoms of boron, phosphorus or arsenic are stripped of an electron, sorted by mass with a magnet, and accelerated through tens of thousands of volts towards the slice. They crash into the crystal and stop at a depth that depends on their energy, leaving damage that a short bake repairs. Resist or oxide blocks the beam wherever it is not wanted, and the dose can be tiny, around a hundred billion atoms on each square centimetre, just enough to nudge a transistor's threshold voltage.`,history:`William Shockley filed a patent on doping semiconductors with a beam of ions in 1954. Through the 1960s researchers at Bell Labs, Hughes and elsewhere built experimental implanters and showed that implanted transistors worked. Mostek, founded in Texas in 1969, was among the first companies to use implantation in production, to set the thresholds of its MOS chips. Commercial implanters from firms such as Extrion appeared in the early 1970s. By the late 1970s implantation had replaced furnace diffusion for most doping steps in MOS chips.`,facts:[`Implanters of the 1970s typically fired ions with energies of about 10,000 to 200,000 electronvolts.`,`Boron ions in a 1970s implanter struck the silicon at hundreds of kilometres a second.`]},"tech:projalign":{intro:`A projection aligner prints the mask pattern onto the slice without the mask ever touching it. Instead of pressing glass against resist, which scratched both and picked up dust, it focuses an image of the mask onto the slice with a system of curved mirrors. Masks last far longer, and fewer chips are spoiled by flaws copied from slice to slice.`,how:`In contact printing, every press left particles and scratches on the mask, so masks wore out quickly and their flaws repeated on every slice. The projection aligner keeps the mask a safe distance away. Its mirrors image a narrow arc-shaped band of the mask at full size, and the mask and slice sweep through that band together, so the whole slice is exposed in one smooth scan. Mirrors focus every colour of light at the same point, avoiding the colour blur of lenses.`,history:`Perkin-Elmer, a US optics company known for telescopes and military cameras, introduced the Micralign projection aligner in 1973. Its optics, designed by Abe Offner, used two concentric curved mirrors to image a ring-shaped field without distortion (1973). By the late 1970s Micraligns were in most of the world's chip factories, and yields rose as mask damage stopped. MOS Technology used them in 1975 to make its cheap 6502 microprocessor with few defects. Steppers began to replace projection aligners in the early 1980s, as lines shrank below about 2 micrometres.`,facts:[`Perkin-Elmer sold more than 2,000 Micraligns from 1973 onwards.`,`Early Micraligns of the 1970s printed lines down to about 2 to 3 micrometres.`]},"tech:p6um":{intro:`Six-micrometre NMOS is a generation of chip-making that builds logic from n-channel MOS transistors with features about six micrometres across, roughly a tenth of a hair's width. In n-channel transistors the current is carried by electrons, which move two to three times faster than the holes of earlier p-channel chips. It makes chips fast and dense enough for a useful computer on a handful of parts.`,how:`Electrons are more easily upset by stray charges near the silicon surface, so NMOS only became practical once oxides were clean and ion implantation could set threshold voltages exactly. A typical process uses silicon gates, a handful of masks and one layer of aluminium wiring. Some designs add 'depletion-mode' load transistors, made by implanting the channel, which switch faster than resistors and can let a chip run on a single 5-volt supply.`,history:`Intel's 8080 microprocessor, made with n-channel silicon-gate MOS, went on sale in April 1974 for $360, roughly ten times as fast as its p-channel 8008. Motorola's 6800, also launched in 1974, ran from a single 5-volt supply. Four-kilobit dynamic memory chips from Texas Instruments, Intel and Mostek reached the market in 1973 and 1974 on processes of this class. MOS Technology made its 6502 of 1975 using projection aligners, getting yields high enough to sell it for $25.`,facts:[`The Intel 8080 of 1974 ran at 2 MHz.`,`The 6502 sold for $25 at its launch in September 1975, a fraction of the 8080's price.`]},"tech:dennardrules":{intro:`Dennard scaling is a recipe for shrinking MOS transistors. If every length, the thickness of the oxide and the supply voltage are all cut by the same factor, the smaller transistor switches faster and uses less power, while the chip's power per square centimetre stays the same. It turns making things smaller into a clear plan instead of guesswork.`,how:`Divide every dimension and the voltage by a factor k. Each transistor then takes one k-squared-th of the area, so k squared as many fit; it switches about k times faster, because charges have less distance to travel; and each uses one k-squared-th of the power. More transistors, running faster but each using less, add up to the same heat for a chip of the same size. The doping inside the silicon has to rise by k to keep the transistors working properly.`,history:`Robert Dennard and colleagues at IBM's research centre in Yorktown Heights, New York, published the scaling rules in the IEEE Journal of Solid-State Circuits in October 1974. Their 1974 paper showed how to design a 1-micrometre transistor from a 5-micrometre one, years before factories could build it. Dennard had already invented the one-transistor memory cell in 1966. In 1975 Gordon Moore predicted that the number of transistors on a chip would double every two years, and Dennard's rules explained why each shrink also brought speed. Through the 1980s chipmakers kept the 5-volt supply for compatibility, so chips ran hotter than the rules intended.`,facts:[`Shrinking every dimension by half fits four times the transistors in the same area (1974 rules).`,`Dennard received the IEEE Medal of Honor in 2009.`]},"tech:p3um":{intro:`Three-micrometre HMOS, high-performance MOS, is one of the first chip processes designed by following Dennard's scaling recipe: thinner oxide, shorter channels and finer lines, all shrunk together. Its transistors are roughly twice as fast as the six-micrometre generation and about four times as dense, which makes 16-bit microprocessors and fast static memories practical on single chips.`,how:`HMOS uses n-channel silicon-gate transistors with depletion-mode loads, a gate oxide much thinner than before and channels about three micrometres long. Projection aligners print the finer patterns, and ion implantation sets the thresholds. Chips run from a single 5-volt supply, sparing designers the extra power rails older chips needed. Smaller transistors move less charge each time they switch, so speed rises while the power of each gate falls.`,history:`Intel introduced HMOS in 1977 with the 2147, a 4-kilobit static memory fast enough to compete with bipolar chips. Intel's 8086 microprocessor, made in HMOS, followed in June 1978 with about 29,000 transistors. Motorola used a similar process for its 68000 of 1979. The 16-kilobit and 64-kilobit dynamic memories of 1978 to 1980 were built on processes of this class. In 1981 IBM chose the 8088, an HMOS version of the 8086 with an 8-bit outside bus, for its Personal Computer.`,facts:[`The Intel 8086 of 1978 held about 29,000 transistors and first ran at 5 MHz.`,`Motorola's 68000 of 1979 was named for its roughly 68,000 transistor sites.`]},"tech:plasmaetch":{intro:`Plasma etching cuts patterns into a chip's layers with a glowing, electrically charged gas instead of a bath of acid. In reactive-ion etching, ions are pulled straight down onto the slice, so they cut vertical walls exactly the shape of the mask. Liquid acid eats sideways as fast as it eats down, which blurs lines once they are only a few micrometres wide.`,how:`The slice sits on an electrode in a vacuum chamber with a little gas such as carbon tetrafluoride. A radio-frequency field strips electrons from the gas and makes a plasma, the glowing state of matter in a neon sign. Its reactive fragments combine with silicon or oxide to form gases that are pumped away, while ions accelerated towards the electrode strike straight down and drive the etching vertically. Different gases are chosen to eat one material and spare the one beneath.`,history:`Chipmakers began using oxygen plasmas to strip away used photoresist around 1970, a cleaner job than strong acids. Reactive-ion etching, in which the slice sits on the powered electrode so ions bombard it vertically, was developed in the mid-1970s at IBM, Bell Labs and Japanese laboratories. Lam Research, founded by David Lam in 1980, sold its first single-wafer plasma etcher, the AutoEtch, in 1981. Applied Materials launched its AME 8100 batch etcher in 1981. By the mid-1980s dry etching had replaced wet etching for nearly every fine pattern on leading chips.`,facts:[`Wet etching of the 1970s undercut the mask by about as much as it etched downwards.`,`Lam Research was founded in 1980 and shipped its first etcher in 1981.`]},"tech:stepper":{intro:`A wafer stepper prints chips one field at a time. It shines blue-violet light through a mask several times larger than the chip and through a large, precise lens that shrinks the image, flashes one field, then moves the slice and flashes the next. Because the mask is bigger than the image, its flaws and errors shrink too, and each field can be lined up separately.`,how:`The light comes from a mercury lamp filtered to its 'g-line', at 436 nanometres. A lens of a dozen or more ground glass elements reduces the mask, called a reticle, by five or ten times. The slice rides on a stage measured by laser interferometers, which place it to a fraction of a micrometre between flashes. The smallest line it can print depends on the wavelength divided by the lens's numerical aperture, a measure of how wide a cone of light it gathers.`,history:`GCA, an American company whose David Mann division made the step-and-repeat cameras used to build masks, delivered the first commercial wafer stepper, the DSW 4800, in 1978. Nikon launched its NSR-1010G stepper in 1980 and quickly won Japanese memory makers. ASML was founded in the Netherlands in 1984 as a joint venture of Philips and ASM International to make steppers. Nikon passed GCA as the biggest stepper supplier in the mid-1980s, and GCA was sold to General Signal in 1988.`,facts:[`The g-line used by steppers from 1978 is violet-blue light at 436 nanometres.`,`Nikon's NSR-1010G of 1980 shrank the mask image ten times.`]},"tech:spc":{intro:`Total quality control is a way of running a factory in which every step is measured, every defect is traced back to its cause, and the cause is fixed, rather than bad products simply being thrown out at the end. In chip-making it means charting each machine's readings, keeping the process steady and asking every worker to help find problems.`,how:`Statistical process control plots measurements, such as the thickness of an oxide layer, on charts with limits worked out from the process's normal variation. A point outside the limits, or a steady drift in one direction, flags a machine before it starts making bad chips. Teams then hunt down the root cause. Steady processes give higher yields, and chips that leave the factory with fewer hidden weaknesses fail less often in customers' hands.`,history:`Walter Shewhart at Bell Labs invented the control chart in 1924. W Edwards Deming taught statistical quality control to Japanese engineers and managers in 1950, and Japan named its top quality award, the Deming Prize, after him in 1951. In March 1980 Richard Anderson of Hewlett-Packard reported that 16-kilobit memories from Japanese makers failed far less often than American ones, both in HP's tests and in use. By 1986 Japanese companies made most of the world's memory chips. In 1987 the US government and 14 chipmakers set up Sematech in Austin, Texas, partly to catch up in manufacturing.`,facts:[`Shewhart drew the first control chart in a memo dated 16 May 1924.`,`The Deming Prize was first awarded in 1951.`,`Sematech began in 1987 with 14 member companies and about $100 million a year from the US government.`]},"tech:p15um":{intro:`The 1.5-micrometre process prints the smallest lines on a chip about one and a half thousandths of a millimetre wide. Steppers that shrink the mask image and plasma etching that cuts straight walls together make it possible. It packs over a hundred thousand transistors onto a microprocessor and a quarter of a million bits onto a memory chip.`,how:`At this size the old methods run out: full-size printing cannot resolve the lines reliably, and acid would undercut them. Each layer is exposed one field at a time by a stepper, then etched with reactive ions. Some designs carry two layers of metal wiring. The lines are so close that a single speck of dust a few micrometres across can bridge them, so cleanrooms must be cleaner again.`,history:`Intel launched the 80286 in February 1982, with about 134,000 transistors on a chip made in a 1.5-micrometre process. IBM built the 286 into its PC/AT, launched in August 1984. Japanese makers such as Hitachi and NEC were producing 256-kilobit memory chips on processes of this class by 1984. Intel's 386, launched in October 1985, held 275,000 transistors on a 1.5-micrometre CMOS process.`,facts:[`The 80286 of 1982 first ran at 6 and 8 MHz.`,`The Intel 386 of 1985 held 275,000 transistors.`]},"tech:cmosvlsi":{intro:`CMOS VLSI means building very large-scale integrated chips, with hundreds of thousands of transistors, entirely in CMOS. NMOS logic wastes current through its load transistors whenever a gate's output is low, and with so many gates on one chip the heat becomes a serious problem. CMOS gates draw power only when they switch, and once transistors are small they are fast enough to win.`,how:`A CMOS process forms an n-well or p-well for one transistor type, or both in a 'twin-tub' process, so n-channel and p-channel transistors sit side by side. Designers must guard against latch-up, in which a hidden pair of junctions turns on and shorts the supply; epitaxial wafers and guard rings help. In return, power falls several times at the same speed, chips need cheaper packages and cooling, and the same design can run on batteries.`,history:`Hitachi and other Japanese makers showed in the early 1980s that CMOS static memories could be both fast and dense. Bell Labs described a twin-tub CMOS process, with separate wells for both transistor types, in 1980. Intel moved its microprocessors to CMOS with its CHMOS III process for the 386 in 1985. Toshiba and others made 1-megabit memory chips in CMOS from 1986. By the late 1980s nearly all new microprocessors and memories were designed in CMOS.`,facts:[`Bell Labs presented its twin-tub CMOS process in 1980.`,`Hitachi's HM6116 CMOS static memory of the early 1980s stored 16 kilobits.`]},"tech:p1um":{intro:`The 1-micrometre process makes a chip's finest features about a thousandth of a millimetre wide. It is the generation at which a single chip holds around a million transistors, whether as a megabit of memory or as a complete 32-bit processor with its cache and maths unit. Nearly every leading chip at this size is made in CMOS with stepper lithography.`,how:`Gate oxide is a few tens of nanometres thick. Two or three layers of aluminium wiring are stacked over the transistors, separated by glass and joined by small plugs called vias. Memory makers fold capacitors into trenches or stacks to fit them into shrinking cells. Every step is checked statistically, and the best factories keep their air close to Class 10, with no more than ten fine particles in a cubic foot.`,history:`Toshiba and other Japanese makers began mass-producing 1-megabit memory chips in 1986 on processes of about a micrometre. By 1987 Japanese companies made about three quarters of the world's dynamic memory chips. Intel's i486, announced in April 1989, put about 1.2 million transistors on a chip made in a 1-micrometre process. The 486 combined the processor, a cache and a floating-point unit on that one chip in 1989.`,facts:[`The i486 of 1989 held about 1.2 million transistors and first ran at 25 MHz.`,`A 1-megabit memory chip of 1986 stored 128 kilobytes.`]},"tech:iline":{intro:`An i-line stepper prints with the mercury lamp's 'i-line', ultraviolet light at 365 nanometres, instead of the 436-nanometre g-line. The finest line a lens can print grows with the wavelength, so shorter light means smaller features. With improved resists and wider lenses, these machines print lines below half a micrometre.`,how:`Ordinary optical glass absorbs much of this ultraviolet, so the lens is built from special glasses that let it through. Resists are re-tuned for the shorter wavelength. The lens's numerical aperture rises to about 0.5 or 0.6, and its depth of focus shrinks, so the slice must be very flat and held exactly level. Phase-shifting masks, which delay the light passing through alternate openings, squeeze out a little more resolution.`,history:`Marc Levenson at IBM published the idea of phase-shifting masks in 1982. Nikon and Canon of Japan brought i-line steppers into production around 1988 to 1990, with the Dutch firm ASML close behind. These i-line machines printed the critical layers of 16-megabit memories in the early 1990s. They were used for the critical layers of the 600 and 350 nanometre generations in the mid-1990s, and for less demanding layers long after that.`,facts:[`The i-line of 1988 steppers is invisible ultraviolet light at 365 nanometres.`,`i-line steppers printed lines down to about 0.35 micrometres by 1995.`]},"tech:cmp":{intro:`Chemical-mechanical polishing grinds and dissolves a chip's surface flat between layers, using a spinning pad and a liquid full of fine abrasive particles. Each layer of wiring leaves hills and valleys that the next layer must cover, and steppers can only focus on a very thin band of depth. Polishing flat lets chips stack many layers of wiring.`,how:`The slice is held face down by a carrier and pressed onto a rotating polyurethane pad while a slurry flows over it. Chemicals in the slurry soften the top of the oxide or metal, and particles of silica or alumina, far smaller than a micrometre, scrape the softened skin away. High points are pressed harder, so they wear first, and the surface levels out. The hard parts are knowing when to stop and cleaning off every particle afterwards.`,history:`Engineers at IBM's East Fishkill plant in New York, including Klaus Beyer, developed chemical-mechanical polishing of oxide layers in the mid-1980s, borrowing from the polishing of bare wafers. IBM was using it in production by the late 1980s. Intel and other chipmakers adopted it in the early 1990s. By the 350-nanometre generation of about 1995, nearly every advanced logic chip was polished between layers. IBM's copper wiring of 1997 depended entirely on polishing away the unwanted metal.`,facts:[`Stepper depth of focus had shrunk to about a micrometre by 1990.`,`IBM's first copper chips of 1998 carried six layers of wiring.`]},"tech:p800":{intro:`The 800-nanometre process, also called 0.8 micrometres, often adds bipolar transistors to a CMOS chip, a mix called BiCMOS. Bipolar transistors drive heavy loads such as long wires and output pins far faster than small MOS transistors, while CMOS handles dense logic and memory at low power. Putting both on one chip gives speed where it counts without the heat of an all-bipolar design.`,how:`A BiCMOS process adds a few extra steps to CMOS, such as a buried layer and a collector contact, so that bipolar transistors can be built beside the MOS ones. Designers use them on the critical paths: clock drivers, memory sense amplifiers and buses. The costs are extra masks, more complex design rules and a supply voltage that is hard to lower, because bipolar transistors need a minimum voltage to work.`,history:`Hitachi and other makers built fast static memories in BiCMOS in the mid-1980s. Four-megabit dynamic memories, among the first with three-dimensional trench or stacked capacitors, reached the market in 1989 and 1990 on processes near 0.8 micrometres. Intel's first Pentium, launched in March 1993, was made in 0.8-micrometre BiCMOS with 3.1 million transistors and ran at 60 or 66 MHz. The 1993 Pentium ran on 5 volts and used about 15 watts, very hot for a desktop chip, which pushed Intel towards a lower supply voltage.`,facts:[`The first Pentium of 1993 held 3.1 million transistors on a die of about 294 square millimetres.`,`A 4-megabit memory chip of 1990 stored half a megabyte.`]},"tech:wafer200":{intro:`A 200-millimetre wafer is a silicon disc eight inches across. Each processing step treats a whole wafer at once, so a bigger disc yields more chips for nearly the same effort. The catch is that every furnace, stepper, etcher and wafer-handling robot has to be redesigned and rebuilt for the larger size, so a new size means a new generation of factories.`,how:`Silicon ingots grow fatter and heavier in the crystal puller, and slicing, polishing and keeping such a large disc perfectly flat and free of strain is harder. Chips at the edge are lost whatever the size, so a bigger wafer wastes a smaller share of its area. A factory built around the new tools costs more but makes each chip cheaper, and the industry must agree standard sizes and carriers so that toolmakers can build for them.`,history:`Silicon wafers grew from about one inch across in 1960 to 100 millimetres by 1975 and 150 millimetres by about 1983. The first 200-millimetre production lines started up around 1990, and by 1992 the size was the standard for new leading-edge factories. By the mid-1990s a new 200-millimetre factory cost around a billion dollars. Older 200-millimetre lines kept running in the 2000s, making simpler chips such as power and sensor parts.`,facts:[`A 200 mm wafer of the 1990s was standardised at about 0.725 millimetres thick.`,`A one-inch wafer of 1960 had about a sixtieth of the area of a 200 mm wafer.`]},"tech:p600":{intro:`The 600-nanometre process, 0.6 micrometres, is the generation at which mainstream chips leave the old 5-volt supply behind and run on 3.3 volts. Following Dennard's recipe at last, the lower voltage keeps the heat of ever more transistors under control, and battery-powered laptops gain most. Lines are printed with i-line steppers, with more layers of metal wiring above.`,how:`Shorter channels and thinner gate oxide cannot stand 5 volts for long: fast electrons damage the oxide. A 3.3-volt supply keeps the electric field safe. Switching power falls with the square of voltage, so a chip uses well under half as much at 3.3 volts as at 5. Chips that must talk to older 5-volt parts carry special input and output circuits, and motherboards gain regulators to provide the new level.`,history:`Intel's second-generation Pentium, the P54C, appeared in March 1994 at 90 and 100 MHz, running on 3.3 volts and made in a 0.6-micrometre BiCMOS process. The PowerPC 601, designed by IBM, Motorola and Apple, went into Apple's first Power Macintosh computers in March 1994. Sixteen-megabit memory chips made on processes of this class became the mainstream memory density in about 1995. By 1995 most new laptop and desktop processors ran on 3.3 volts or less.`,facts:[`Cutting the supply from 5 to 3.3 volts cut switching power by more than half (1994).`,`The PowerPC 601 of 1994 held about 2.8 million transistors.`]},"tech:scanner":{intro:`A step-and-scan system, or scanner, exposes each chip through a narrow slit of light while the mask and the wafer glide past it in opposite directions, then steps to the next field and scans again. Only a thin strip of the lens has to be perfect, rather than its whole round field, so a scanner can print larger chips with sharper, more even images than a stepper.`,how:`The lens shrinks the image four times, so the mask must move four times faster than the wafer, and both stages keep exactly in step to within a few nanometres while moving at speed. Errors in the lens are averaged out along the scan. Small corrections are made continuously during each sweep, for example to keep the image in focus as the wafer's surface rises and falls by fractions of a micrometre.`,history:`Perkin-Elmer developed the first step-and-scan system, the Micrascan, which the Silicon Valley Group took over when it bought Perkin-Elmer's lithography business in 1990. ASML launched its PAS 5500 range in 1991 as a stepper and added scanning models in the mid-1990s. By the late 1990s scanners had replaced steppers for the critical layers of leading chips. In 2001 ASML bought the Silicon Valley Group, maker of the Micrascan, for about $1.6 billion.`,facts:[`Scanners of the 1990s shrank the mask image four times, where steppers usually shrank it five.`,`ASML bought the Silicon Valley Group for about $1.6 billion in 2001.`]},"tech:p350":{intro:`The 350-nanometre process is a chip generation with transistor gates about a third of a micrometre long and four or five layers of metal wiring, polished flat between them. It is the size at which processors pass several million transistors and memory chips reach 64 megabits, while supply voltages fall towards 3.3 volts and below.`,how:`Critical layers are printed with i-line steppers pushed to their limit, or with the first deep-ultraviolet machines. Chemical-mechanical polishing flattens every layer, and tungsten plugs fill the narrow holes between metal levels. Short channels let current leak even when a transistor is off, so engineers tune the doping in small pockets beside the source and drain to hold it back.`,history:`Intel moved its Pentium to a 0.35-micrometre process in 1995, reaching 133 MHz. The Pentium Pro of November 1995 used 0.35 micrometres for its faster versions. AMD's K6 processor of April 1997 was made in 0.35 micrometres with 8.8 million transistors. Intel's Pentium II, code-named Klamath, launched in May 1997 at 233 to 300 MHz on the same process. Sixty-four-megabit memory chips came into volume use in 1997 and 1998.`,facts:[`The AMD K6 of 1997 held 8.8 million transistors.`,`The first Pentium II of 1997 held 7.5 million transistors on a cartridge with its cache.`]},"tech:krf":{intro:`A krypton-fluoride excimer laser makes deep-ultraviolet light at 248 nanometres, much shorter than the mercury lamp's lines. Paired with a new kind of photoresist, it lets steppers and scanners print features a quarter of a micrometre wide and smaller. Its light is invisible and so energetic that ordinary glass would absorb it, so the lenses are made of fused silica.`,how:`An excimer is a molecule that exists only in an excited state: an electric discharge through krypton and fluorine briefly binds them, and when the pair falls apart it gives off an ultraviolet photon. The laser fires thousands of short pulses each second. Its light is weak by chip-making standards, so resists are 'chemically amplified': each photon releases one acid molecule, and during a bake each acid unlocks hundreds of chemical groups in the resist.`,history:`Kanti Jain at IBM proposed using excimer lasers for lithography in 1982. Hiroshi Ito, Grant Willson and Jean Fréchet, working with IBM, invented chemically amplified resists in 1982. IBM put chemically amplified resists into production in the mid-1980s, at first with mercury-lamp light. Cymer, founded in San Diego in 1986, became the main supplier of KrF lasers for lithography. KrF machines entered volume production in 1994 and 1995 and printed the critical layers of the 250, 180 and 130 nanometre generations.`,facts:[`A KrF photon of 248 nm carries about 5 electronvolts, enough to break many chemical bonds (1995).`,`KrF lithography lasers of the late 1990s pulsed about a thousand times a second or more.`]},"tech:p250":{intro:`The 250-nanometre process, a quarter of a micrometre, is the first generation whose critical layers are printed with deep-ultraviolet laser light. Supply voltage falls to about 2.5 volts or less, and chips carry around ten million transistors. Processors made this way run at several hundred megahertz, and graphics chips draw millions of textured triangles a second.`,how:`KrF scanners print gates about a quarter of a micrometre long, and transistors are separated by shallow trenches filled with oxide, more compact than the old method of growing thick oxide between them. Five or six layers of aluminium wiring, polished flat, connect them. Wires now slow a chip almost as much as transistors do, because their resistance and the capacitance between neighbours grow as they shrink.`,history:`Intel began shipping 0.25-micrometre processors with a mobile Pentium MMX in 1997, and the Pentium II code-named Deschutes followed in January 1998 at 333 MHz. AMD's K6-2 of May 1998 was made at 0.25 micrometres and added 3DNow! instructions for 3D maths. Intel's Pentium III, code-named Katmai, launched in February 1999 and reached 600 MHz on the same process. Sony's PlayStation 2, launched in Japan in March 2000, used an Emotion Engine processor first made at 0.25 micrometres.`,facts:[`The Deschutes Pentium II of 1998 ran its core on about 2 volts.`,`The Katmai Pentium III of 1999 held 9.5 million transistors.`]},"tech:copper":{intro:`Copper wiring replaces aluminium in the metal layers that join a chip's transistors. Copper carries current with less resistance and stands up far better to electromigration, the slow drift of metal atoms under heavy current that wears thin wires through. But copper atoms poison silicon transistors and cannot be etched with a plasma, so copper wiring has to be built in a new way.`,how:`In the damascene method, named after the inlaid metalwork of Damascus, trenches and holes are etched into the insulating glass first. A thin barrier, often of tantalum, lines them to keep copper out of the silicon; a seed layer is sputtered on, and copper is electroplated to overfill the trenches, much as jewellery is plated. Polishing then removes all the copper above the surface, leaving metal only in the inlaid lines.`,history:`IBM announced in September 1997 that it had made copper wiring work on chips, after years of research at its laboratories in New York. IBM shipped its first copper-wired PowerPC processors in 1998. AMD's Fab 30 in Dresden, Germany, made copper-wired Athlon processors from 2000. Intel moved to copper with its 130-nanometre process in 2001. By 2003 copper was standard in every leading-edge logic process.`,facts:[`Copper's resistivity is about 1.7 microhm-centimetres, against about 2.7 for aluminium (1997).`,`IBM announced its copper chip process in September 1997.`]},"tech:opc":{intro:`Optical proximity correction deliberately distorts the pattern on a mask so that the image it prints comes out right. Once features are smaller than the wavelength of the light, corners round off, line ends shrink back, and isolated lines print thinner than crowded ones. OPC adds tiny serifs, hammerheads and nudged edges that cancel these effects in advance.`,how:`Software models how light diffracts through the lens and how the resist reacts, then simulates the printed result of every shape on the chip. Edges are moved in small steps until the simulated print matches the designer's intended layout. Extra 'scattering bars', too thin to print themselves, are added beside lonely lines so they behave like crowded ones. Phase-shift masks, which change the thickness of the glass to flip the light's phase, sharpen edges further.`,history:`Researchers at IBM, Bell Labs and elsewhere studied ways to correct optical proximity effects in the 1980s. Rule-based OPC, which applies fixed corrections to certain shapes, entered production for 250 and 180 nanometre chips in the late 1990s. By the 130-nanometre generation of 2001, model-based OPC, which simulates the whole layout, was standard for critical layers. In 2003 Synopsys bought Numerical Technologies, a pioneer of phase-shift masks and OPC software, for about $250 million.`,facts:[`In 1999 printed lines of 180 nm were about three quarters of the 248 nm wavelength used.`,`By 2001, 130 nm lines were barely half the wavelength of the 248 nm light printing them.`]},"tech:soi":{intro:`Silicon on insulator builds transistors in a very thin film of silicon resting on a layer of glass, instead of in the top of a thick solid wafer. The buried glass stops charge leaking into the bulk of the wafer and cuts the stray capacitance each transistor must charge every time it switches. The chip can then run faster, or at the same speed on less power.`,how:`One way to make the wafers, SIMOX, fires huge doses of oxygen ions into silicon and bakes it so that a layer of oxide forms below the surface. Another, Smart Cut, implants hydrogen into an oxidised wafer, bonds it face down onto a second wafer and splits it along the hydrogen layer, leaving a thin film of silicon on glass. The wafers cost more, and designers must allow for the 'floating body', which makes each transistor's behaviour depend on what it did just before.`,history:`Silicon on sapphire, an earlier relative built on crystal sapphire, was used from the 1960s in radiation-hardened chips for satellites and weapons. Michel Bruel at the French research centre CEA-Leti invented the Smart Cut process in 1991, and Soitec was founded in 1992 to make wafers with it. IBM announced in August 1998 that it had brought silicon on insulator into mainstream processor manufacturing. AMD adopted it for its Opteron and Athlon 64 processors in 2003. The Xbox 360 processor of 2005 and the Cell chip of the PlayStation 3 in 2006, both made by IBM, were built on it.`,facts:[`Soitec was founded near Grenoble, France, in 1992.`,`AMD's Athlon 64 launched in September 2003 on a 130 nm silicon-on-insulator process.`]},"tech:p180":{intro:`The 180-nanometre process, 0.18 micrometres, is printed with deep-ultraviolet light whose wavelength is longer than the lines being drawn, helped by corrections on the mask. Supply voltage falls to around 1.8 volts or below, and a single processor holds tens of millions of transistors, enough to bring the second-level cache onto the same chip as the processor.`,how:`KrF scanners with optical proximity correction print the critical layers. Six or more levels of wiring, in aluminium or the new copper, sit above transistors separated by shallow oxide-filled trenches. Gate oxide is only a few nanometres thick. With fast cache memory on the same die, a processor's speed depends as much on how quickly it can reach its data as on its logic.`,history:`Intel's Pentium III code-named Coppermine, launched in October 1999, was made at 180 nanometres and carried its 256-kilobyte second-level cache on the chip. AMD's Athlon broke the 1 GHz barrier on 6 March 2000, two days ahead of Intel's 1 GHz Pentium III. NVIDIA's GeForce2 graphics chips of 2000 were made in the same generation. Intel's first Pentium 4, Willamette, launched in November 2000 at 1.4 and 1.5 GHz on a 180-nanometre process.`,facts:[`The Coppermine Pentium III of 1999 held about 28 million transistors.`,`The first Pentium 4 of 2000 held about 42 million transistors.`]},"tech:wafer300":{intro:`A 300-millimetre wafer is a silicon disc about twelve inches across, the size chosen for a whole new generation of chip factories. Each disc holds well over twice as many chips as a 200-millimetre wafer. It is too heavy, valuable and fragile to handle by hand, so the factory is built around automated transport, with every tool redesigned for the new size.`,how:`Wafers travel in sealed front-opening pods, FOUPs, holding 25 each, carried between tools by vehicles running on overhead rails, so only the inside of the pods and machines must be spotless. The larger diameter cuts the share of chips wasted at the edge, and fewer, larger batches mean less labour per chip. The cost is a factory price of several billion dollars and years of work on new standards.`,history:`In the mid-1990s groups such as Sematech's International 300 mm Initiative and Japan's Selete agreed standards for the new tools. In 1999 Infineon, the former chip arm of Siemens, and Motorola ran a pilot 300-millimetre line called Semiconductor300 in Dresden. Infineon began volume production on 300-millimetre wafers in Dresden in 2001. Intel opened its first 300-millimetre factories in Oregon and New Mexico in 2002. By the mid-2000s nearly all leading-edge logic and memory chips were made on 300-millimetre wafers.`,facts:[`A 300 mm wafer has about 70,700 square millimetres of area (2001).`,`Standard 300 mm wafers of 2001 were about 0.775 millimetres thick.`,`A FOUP of 2001 carried 25 wafers.`]},"tech:arf":{intro:`An argon-fluoride excimer laser makes ultraviolet light at 193 nanometres, about a fifth shorter than the krypton-fluoride laser's 248. Shorter light prints smaller features, so ArF scanners take over the critical layers of chips as features shrink towards 100 nanometres. The light is so energetic that oxygen in the air absorbs it and most lens materials darken under it.`,how:`The laser works like the KrF type, with argon in place of krypton. The beam path is flushed with nitrogen, and lenses are made of very pure fused silica, with calcium fluoride crystal for some elements, since few materials pass this light without damage. Resists made for 248 nanometres absorb this light too strongly, so chemists designed new ones based on acrylic plastics with bulky ring-shaped groups that resist plasma etching.`,history:`In the late 1980s researchers at MIT's Lincoln Laboratory showed that printing with 193-nanometre light could work. ASML, Nikon and Canon shipped the first production ArF scanners around 2000 and 2001, used at first for a few layers of 130-nanometre chips. Intel had spent heavily on 157-nanometre fluorine lasers as the next step, but dropped them in May 2003 in favour of 193-nanometre light. From the 90-nanometre generation of 2004, all the most critical layers were printed with 193-nanometre light.`,facts:[`An ArF photon of 193 nm carries about 6.4 electronvolts (2001).`,`Intel abandoned 157 nm lithography in 2003.`]},"tech:p130":{intro:`The 130-nanometre process is a chip generation with transistor gates only about 70 nanometres long and six or more layers of copper wiring. Supply voltages fall to around 1.5 volts. A processor now holds tens of millions of transistors, many of them in fast cache memory, and the gate insulator has thinned to just a few layers of atoms.`,how:`Gates are printed longer than they end up: a trimming etch thins the resist so that the finished gate is narrower than any line the scanner could draw directly. The gate oxide is only about 1.5 nanometres thick, and electrons begin to tunnel straight through it, so even idle chips leak current. Copper wires, with glass of lower dielectric constant between them, carry signals across the chip.`,history:`Intel's Pentium III code-named Tualatin, its first 130-nanometre processor, launched in 2001. The Pentium 4 code-named Northwood followed in January 2002 with 512 kilobytes of cache, reaching 2.2 GHz. AMD moved its Athlon XP to 130 nanometres in 2002, and its Athlon 64 of 2003 used 130 nanometres on silicon on insulator. NVIDIA blamed part of the delay of its GeForce FX, which reached shops in 2003, on the difficulties of TSMC's new 130-nanometre process.`,facts:[`The Northwood Pentium 4 of 2002 held 55 million transistors on 146 square millimetres.`,`Intel's 130 nm transistors of 2001 had gate oxide about 1.5 nm thick.`]},"tech:lowk":{intro:`Low-k dielectrics are insulating materials, placed between a chip's wires, that store less electric charge than ordinary silicon dioxide. Wires side by side act like tiny capacitors, and each time a signal changes that stray charge must be filled or drained, which wastes time and power. A lower 'k', the dielectric constant, cuts this coupling, so signals move faster and neighbouring wires disturb each other less.`,how:`Silicon dioxide has a dielectric constant of about 4. Adding fluorine lowers it to about 3.6, and carbon-doped oxide, a glass laced with carbon and full of tiny pores, brings it below 3. The catch is strength: the lighter, more porous films are soft, crack under polishing, soak up moisture and peel away from copper, so the processes and packaging around them must be gentler.`,history:`Fluorine-doped oxide, with a k of around 3.6, entered production in the late 1990s. Carbon-doped oxides, with k near 3, went into production at the 130 and 90 nanometre generations in 2003 and 2004, and Intel adopted one for its 90-nanometre chips in 2004. TSMC's troubles with low-k films at 130 nanometres in 2002 showed how hard the fragile materials were to make reliable. In 2007 IBM announced chips with air gaps between some wires, since air has the lowest k of all. By about 2010 porous films with k near 2.5 insulated the densest wiring layers.`,facts:[`Air has a dielectric constant of 1; IBM announced chips with air gaps between wires in 2007.`,`Intel's 90 nm carbon-doped oxide of 2004 had a k of about 2.9.`]},"tech:strained":{intro:`Strained silicon stretches or squeezes the crystal in a transistor's channel so that electrons or holes travel through it faster. A crystal under strain changes the energy landscape the charge moves through, so the carriers are scattered less and seem lighter. Faster carriers give more current from the same transistor, a gain that shrinking alone no longer delivers.`,how:`Electrons, which carry current in n-channel transistors, speed up when the silicon is stretched along the channel; holes, in p-channel ones, speed up when it is squeezed. One method grows silicon-germanium in pockets beside the p-channel: germanium atoms are bigger, so the pockets push on the channel and compress it. Another coats n-channel transistors with a stretched film of silicon nitride that pulls on the silicon beneath. Each transistor type gets its own kind of strain.`,history:`In the early 1990s researchers at MIT, IBM and elsewhere showed that silicon grown on silicon-germanium carried electrons much faster. Intel described its strained-silicon transistors for the 90-nanometre generation in 2002, using silicon-germanium pockets for p-channel transistors and a nitride film for n-channel ones. Intel said in 2002 that the strain raised transistor current by up to about 20 per cent while adding only about 2 per cent to manufacturing cost. Intel shipped strained silicon in its Prescott Pentium 4 in February 2004. IBM and AMD introduced their own strain techniques in 2005.`,facts:[`Germanium atoms are about 4% larger than silicon atoms, the source of the strain (2003).`,`Intel's strained 90 nm transistors of 2004 had gates about 50 nm long.`]},"tech:p90":{intro:`The 90-nanometre process is a chip generation that combines 193-nanometre laser lithography, strained silicon, and copper wiring insulated with low-k glass, on 300-millimetre wafers. Its gate insulator has shrunk to only a few atomic layers, so a noticeable current tunnels through even when transistors are idle. For the first time, wasted leakage becomes a large share of a chip's total power.`,how:`Gates are about 50 nanometres long, and the oxide beneath them about 1.2 nanometres. Each transistor's speed depends on strain built into its channel. Designers now juggle two kinds of power: switching power, which falls with lower voltage, and leakage, which rises steeply as thresholds and oxide shrink. Many chips mix several kinds of transistor: fast, leaky ones for critical paths and slower, frugal ones everywhere else.`,history:`Intel's Pentium 4 code-named Prescott, its first 90-nanometre processor, launched in February 2004 with 125 million transistors. Prescott ran hotter than its predecessor, up to about 115 watts, and Intel cancelled its planned 4 GHz version in October 2004. The Cell processor, designed by Sony, Toshiba and IBM, was unveiled in February 2005 as a 90-nanometre chip with 234 million transistors. NVIDIA's G80 graphics chip, sold as the GeForce 8800 in November 2006, put 681 million transistors on a 90-nanometre die.`,facts:[`The Prescott Pentium 4 of 2004 held 125 million transistors on about 112 square millimetres.`,`Intel's 90 nm gate oxide of 2004 was about 1.2 nm thick.`,`NVIDIA's G80 of 2006 measured about 484 square millimetres.`]},"tech:p65":{intro:`The 65-nanometre process is the generation at which shrinking stops bringing much extra speed. Supply voltage can hardly fall further without transistors leaking too much, so each step mainly brings more transistors on a chip rather than faster clocks. Designers spend the extra transistors on two or more processor cores and large caches instead.`,how:`Gates are about 35 nanometres long, with oxide no thinner than before, about 1.2 nanometres, since thinner would leak too much. Strain from silicon-germanium and nitride films is increased to keep current rising. Chips carry 'sleep' transistors that switch off whole blocks when idle, and clocks are gated so unused circuits do not tick. Memory cells shrink well, so caches grow to several megabytes.`,history:`Intel shipped its first 65-nanometre processors in January 2006, including the Core Duo for laptops. The Core 2 Duo of July 2006 used 65 nanometres to fit two cores and up to 4 megabytes of shared cache into 291 million transistors. AMD moved its Athlon 64 to 65 nanometres in December 2006. The processors of the Xbox 360 and the PlayStation 3 moved to 65 nanometres in 2007 and 2008, cutting the consoles' power and cost. NVIDIA's GT200 of June 2008 held 1.4 billion transistors at 65 nanometres.`,facts:[`The Core 2 Duo code-named Conroe of 2006 measured about 143 square millimetres.`,`NVIDIA's GT200 of 2008 measured about 576 square millimetres.`]},"tech:immersion":{intro:`Immersion lithography puts a thin layer of ultrapure water between the last lens of a scanner and the wafer. Water bends light more strongly than air, so the lens can focus a wider cone of rays onto the wafer, and wider cones print finer detail. With the same 193-nanometre laser, it prints lines roughly 30 per cent smaller than a dry scanner can.`,how:`Water's refractive index at 193 nanometres is about 1.44, which lets a lens reach a numerical aperture above 1, impossible in air. A nozzle ring around the lens holds a puddle of water about a millimetre deep under it as the wafer races beneath, without spilling or leaving droplets behind. Bubbles, temperature changes and chemicals leaching out of the resist all distort the image, so protective topcoats and special resists keep water and resist apart.`,history:`Microscope makers had used oil between lens and specimen to see finer detail since 1878, when Ernst Abbe designed an oil-immersion lens for Carl Zeiss. Burn Lin, an engineer at TSMC in Taiwan, argued in 2002 that 193-nanometre light under water would beat the industry's planned move to 157-nanometre lasers. ASML shipped its first immersion scanners for development in 2004, and its XT:1700i, with a numerical aperture of 1.2, entered production in 2006. ASML's XT:1900i of 2007 reached 1.35, about the practical limit with water.`,facts:[`Water's refractive index at 193 nm is about 1.44 (2006).`,`ASML's XT:1900i of 2007 reached a numerical aperture of 1.35.`]},"tech:hkmg":{intro:`A high-k metal gate replaces two of the oldest parts of the MOS transistor: the gate insulator of silicon dioxide and the silicon gate above it. The new insulator, based on hafnium oxide, holds charge so well that it can be several times thicker for the same effect, which stops electrons tunnelling through. A metal gate replaces silicon because the two new partners work badly together.`,how:`A gate's grip on the channel depends on its capacitance, set by the insulator's dielectric constant, k, divided by its thickness. Hafnium oxide's k is about 25, against about 4 for silicon dioxide, so a film several times thicker gives the same grip with far less leakage. Silicon gates on hafnium oxide trap charge and slow the transistor, so metals tuned separately for n-channel and p-channel transistors take their place. In Intel's 'gate-last' method, a dummy silicon gate is removed after the hottest steps and metal is filled in.`,history:`Chipmakers began searching for a high-k replacement for silicon dioxide in the late 1990s as gate oxide approached a nanometre and a half. Intel announced high-k metal gates in January 2007, and Gordon Moore called it the biggest change in transistor technology since the late 1960s. IBM and its partners announced their own high-k metal gates the same week in January 2007. Intel shipped its first high-k metal gate chips, the 45-nanometre Penryn processors, in November 2007. TSMC brought high-k metal gates to its 28-nanometre process in 2011.`,facts:[`Hafnium oxide's dielectric constant is about 25, roughly six times that of silicon dioxide (2007).`,`Hafnium was discovered in Copenhagen in 1923 and named after the city's Latin name, Hafnia.`]},"tech:p45":{intro:`The 45-nanometre process is the generation that brings high-k insulators and metal gates into everyday processors, replacing the silicon gate over silicon dioxide. Its transistors switch faster and leak far less than they would with the old materials, so shrinking can carry on. A processor now holds several hundred million transistors, and a small but complete PC processor fits on a few square millimetres.`,how:`Critical layers are printed with 193-nanometre light, dry or through water. Gates are about 35 nanometres long and sit on hafnium-based insulators that are physically thicker than the old oxide but grip the channel as tightly. Eight or nine layers of copper wiring connect the transistors. With leakage under control again, designers can add more cores and bigger caches without the chip running hot when idle.`,history:`Intel launched its first 45-nanometre processors, the Penryn family, in November 2007. Intel's Atom, a tiny low-power processor for netbooks, appeared in 2008 on a die of about 25 square millimetres. The Core i7, using the Nehalem design with the memory controller built into the processor, followed in November 2008. AMD's first 45-nanometre processors, built on silicon on insulator without high-k gates, appeared in late 2008.`,facts:[`A dual-core Penryn of 2007 held about 410 million transistors.`,`The first Atom of 2008 held about 47 million transistors.`]},"tech:p40":{intro:`The 40-nanometre process is a 'half-node' offered by contract chipmakers, called foundries, which manufacture chips for companies that design them but own no factories. It shrinks a full 45-nanometre generation slightly further, printing its finest lines through water with immersion lithography. Graphics processors and phone chips from many different designers are made on it.`,how:`A foundry half-node takes a standard process and scales its dimensions by about ten per cent, so a design can be moved over with little rework and gains area and cost. The foundry publishes design rules and libraries of tested building blocks, and customers send it finished layouts. Many companies share the same production lines, so a yield problem in the process hits all of them at once.`,history:`TSMC, the Taiwanese foundry founded by Morris Chang in 1987, began making 40-nanometre chips in late 2008. Early yields were poor, and shortages in 2009 held back supplies of AMD's Radeon HD 5870, launched in September 2009. NVIDIA's Fermi chip, sold as the GeForce GTX 480 with about 3 billion transistors, arrived in March 2010 after months of delay on the same process. TSMC dropped its planned 32-nanometre process in 2009, so many designs went straight from 40 to 28 nanometres.`,facts:[`AMD's Radeon HD 5870 of 2009 held about 2.15 billion transistors.`,`TSMC was founded in 1987 with backing from the Taiwanese government and Philips.`]},"tech:doublepat":{intro:`Double patterning prints one layer of a chip in two or more steps when lines are packed too closely for a single exposure to separate them. The layout is split into two sets of shapes, each with gaps wide enough to print. Laid one over the other, they form a pattern twice as dense as the light could draw in a single pass.`,how:`In 'litho-etch, litho-etch', half the lines are exposed and etched into a hard mask, then a second mask prints the lines in between, lined up to within a few nanometres. In self-aligned double patterning, thin films are deposited on the sides of printed lines like coats of paint, the original lines are etched away, and the coatings remain at twice the density. Every extra step costs time, masks and money, and the layout must be split so that no two close neighbours share a mask.`,history:`Memory makers began using self-aligned double patterning for NAND flash, whose regular stripes suit it, around 2008. Intel's 32-nanometre process of 2009 used immersion lithography with extra patterning steps for its tightest layers. TSMC's 20-nanometre process of 2014 was the first foundry process to need double patterning for its wiring. By 2018 self-aligned quadruple patterning, which repeats the trick twice, printed lines a quarter of the original spacing for 10 and 7 nanometre chips.`,facts:[`A water-immersion scanner of 2009 could print lines no closer than about 80 nm apart in one pass.`,`Each double-patterned layer of 2014 needed two masks instead of one.`]},"tech:p32":{intro:`The 32-nanometre process is the second generation of high-k metal gate transistors, with the critical layers of leading processors printed through water. It is dense enough to put a processor's cores, its graphics engine and its memory controller on a single die, so the main chips of a whole PC shrink into one.`,how:`Gates are around 30 nanometres long, and strain and metal gates are refined from the generation before. Wiring is printed with immersion scanners and double patterning on the tightest layers. With room to spare, designers bring the graphics processor and the north bridge, which links the processor to memory, inside the main chip, cutting power and the distances signals must travel.`,history:`Intel's first 32-nanometre processors, the Westmere family, went on sale in January 2010; the Clarkdale versions placed a separate 45-nanometre graphics chip beside the processor in the same package. Sandy Bridge, launched in January 2011, put the cores and graphics on one 32-nanometre die. GlobalFoundries had been formed from AMD's factories in March 2009. AMD's first Fusion processors, the Llano chips made by GlobalFoundries on 32-nanometre silicon on insulator, followed in June 2011.`,facts:[`A quad-core Sandy Bridge of 2011 held 995 million transistors on 216 square millimetres.`,`Intel's six-core Westmere of 2010 held about 1.17 billion transistors.`]},"tech:williams":{intro:`The Williams tube is a computer memory that stores bits as dots of electric charge on the face of a cathode-ray tube, like a small television screen. A bit can be read or written anywhere on the screen in microseconds, without waiting for anything to come round, so it is one of the first random-access memories. The dots fade within a fraction of a second, so they are constantly refreshed.`,how:`An electron beam writes a dot or a dash at each point of a grid, leaving a tiny patch of charge on the screen's inner surface. A metal plate on the outside picks up a small pulse when the beam returns to that spot, and the pulse's shape tells a dot from a dash. Each read wipes the bit, so it is rewritten at once, and the whole screen is swept and rewritten continually. The tubes pick up electrical noise easily and need constant adjustment.`,history:`Freddie Williams and Tom Kilburn developed the storage tube at the University of Manchester in 1946 and 1947. To test it, they built the Manchester Baby, which ran the world's first stored program on 21 June 1948. The Baby's main store in 1948 was a single tube holding 32 words of 32 bits. Ferranti built Williams tubes into the Mark 1, the first commercially sold general-purpose computer, delivered in February 1951. IBM's 701, its first large scientific computer, announced in 1952, used 72 Williams tubes. Magnetic core replaced the tubes in new computers by the mid-1950s.`,facts:[`The Manchester Baby's first program, on 21 June 1948, ran for 52 minutes.`,`The IBM 701 of 1952 kept 2,048 words in its 72 Williams tubes.`]},"tech:delayline":{intro:`A mercury delay line stores bits as pulses of sound travelling through a tube of mercury. A crystal at one end turns electrical pulses into sound, which takes about a millisecond to reach a crystal at the far end that turns it back into electricity. The pulses are cleaned up, amplified and fed back in, so a train of hundreds of bits circulates for as long as the machine runs.`,how:`Sound moves through mercury at about 1.45 kilometres a second, so a tube a metre and a half long holds a pulse for about a millisecond, room for hundreds of bits spaced along it. Mercury is used because it matches the crystals well and carries sound with little loss. The catch is waiting: a bit can only be read as it emerges, so the computer pauses until the word it wants comes round. The tubes are kept warm and steady, since the speed of sound changes with heat.`,history:`In 1944 Presper Eckert, who had built mercury delay lines for radar at the University of Pennsylvania, proposed them as the memory of the EDVAC computer. Maurice Wilkes's EDSAC at Cambridge, storing its program and data in mercury tanks about 1.5 metres long, ran its first program on 6 May 1949. Alan Turing's design for Britain's ACE also relied on delay lines, and its Pilot ACE ran in May 1950. UNIVAC I, delivered to the US Census Bureau in 1951, kept its main memory in mercury tanks. Magnetic core replaced delay lines in new computers from the mid-1950s.`,facts:[`Sound crosses mercury at about 1,450 metres a second (1949).`,`UNIVAC I of 1951 stored 1,000 words of 12 characters each in mercury.`]},"tech:drum":{intro:`A magnetic drum is a metal cylinder coated with magnetic material, spinning steadily past a row of fixed read and write heads. Each head covers one ring, or track, of the drum. It is cheaper and more robust than tubes of mercury or charge, and keeps its contents when the power is off, but the computer must wait for the right spot to come round.`,how:`The drum turns at thousands of revolutions a minute, and each track holds hundreds or thousands of bits recorded as tiny magnetised patches, much as on tape. The average wait is half a turn, a few milliseconds, so programmers learn to place each next instruction where the head will be just as the last one finishes, a craft called optimum coding. Some machines use a drum as their main memory; others keep it as a larger, slower store behind faster memory.`,history:`Engineering Research Associates, founded in 1946 in St Paul, Minnesota, by former US Navy codebreakers, built drum memories for the Navy in the late 1940s. ERA's 1101, completed in 1950, kept its program and data on a magnetic drum. IBM announced its 650 Magnetic Drum Data-Processing Machine in 1953 and delivered the first in December 1954. The 650 was the most widely used computer of the 1950s, with about 2,000 built. Drums served as fast backing stores beside core memory in the 1960s.`,facts:[`The IBM 650's drum of 1954 spun at 12,500 revolutions a minute.`,`The basic IBM 650 drum of 1954 held 2,000 words of ten decimal digits.`]},"tech:tape":{intro:`Magnetic tape stores data as patterns of magnetised spots on a long ribbon wound on reels, much like sound on a tape recorder. A single reel holds as much as many thousands of punched cards and reads far faster. But data can only be read in order: to reach the end of the reel, everything before it must wind past the heads first.`,how:`Several tracks run side by side along the tape, one for each bit of a character plus a check bit, so a whole character is written across the tape at once. Records are separated by blank gaps so the tape can stop and start between them. Drives hang loops of slack tape in vacuum columns, so the heavy reels need not jerk with every start. Business work is done in batches: sort the day's transactions into the same order as the master file and run both reels through together.`,history:`The UNISERVO drive of UNIVAC I, delivered in 1951, was the first tape drive for a commercial computer and used heavy strips of nickel-plated bronze. IBM's 726 of 1952, made for its 701 computer, used lighter plastic tape coated with iron oxide, and vacuum columns that let it start and stop in milliseconds. Half-inch IBM-style tape became the common way to move data between computers in the late 1950s. Nine-track tape, introduced with System/360 in 1964, carried a whole 8-bit byte across the tape at once.`,facts:[`IBM's 726 tape drive of 1952 recorded 100 characters to the inch.`,`Nine-track tape packed 800 characters an inch in 1964, and 6,250 by 1973.`]},"tech:coremem":{intro:`Magnetic-core memory stores each bit in a tiny ring of ferrite, a ceramic magnet, threaded onto a grid of fine wires. A ring magnetised clockwise means one; anticlockwise means zero. Any bit can be reached in a few microseconds, the memory is far more reliable than storage tubes or delay lines, and it keeps its contents when the power is switched off.`,how:`Each core sits where an X wire and a Y wire cross. Half the current needed to flip a core is sent down one X wire and one Y wire, so only the core at their crossing gets enough to flip. A third wire senses whether it flipped, which tells what it held; since reading erases the bit, it is written straight back. A fourth 'inhibit' wire controls writing. The cores are threaded by hand, often by women working under magnifiers.`,history:`Jay Forrester at MIT conceived coincident-current core memory in 1949 while looking for a faster, more reliable store for the Whirlwind computer. Core memory replaced Whirlwind's troublesome storage tubes in August 1953 and doubled the machine's speed. IBM's 704 of 1955 offered up to 32,768 words of core. By the 1960s core was the main memory of nearly every computer, from minicomputers to mainframes. In 1964 IBM paid MIT $13 million to settle the patent on Forrester's design.`,facts:[`IBM paid MIT $13 million in 1964 for the rights to Forrester's core memory patent.`,`By the late 1960s cores were only about half a millimetre across.`]},"tech:diskdrive":{intro:`A hard disk stores data on spinning metal platters coated with magnetic oxide, with a moving arm that carries a head to any track. Unlike tape, any record can be reached in under a second without reading everything before it, so a business can look up one customer's account the moment it is needed. It is the first random-access mass storage.`,how:`The platters spin together on one shaft, and each surface holds many rings of data called tracks. An access arm moves in and out to the right track, and on the first machines up and down to the right platter as well, then waits for the record to come round under the head. A cushion of air keeps the head from scraping the surface. Data is written as tiny magnetised patches, as on tape, but on a rigid disk that can be revisited at will.`,history:`Reynold Johnson's team at IBM's new laboratory in San Jose, California, began work on disk storage in 1952. IBM announced the 305 RAMAC computer and its 350 disk unit in September 1956. The 350 of 1956 had fifty platters 24 inches across, spinning at 1,200 revolutions a minute, and stored about 5 million characters. IBM built about 1,000 RAMAC systems before withdrawing it in 1961. IBM's 1311 of 1962 introduced removable disk packs of six platters holding about 2 million characters each.`,facts:[`The IBM 350 of 1956 took about 600 milliseconds on average to find a record.`,`The 350 disk unit of 1956 weighed about a tonne.`]},"tech:bipolarram":{intro:`Semiconductor RAM stores each bit in a flip-flop, a small circuit of transistors that rests in one of two stable states until told to change. Built from bipolar transistors on integrated circuits, it is much faster than magnetic core but costs far more per bit, so it first serves as a small, fast scratchpad or buffer beside a computer's main memory.`,how:`A flip-flop has two transistors cross-coupled so that each holds the other off or on, and extra transistors connect it to the bit lines for reading and writing. The bit stays put as long as power is applied, with no refreshing, but vanishes when the power goes off. A chip holds a small array of these cells plus decoders that pick one by its address, and an access takes tens of nanoseconds. Bipolar cells draw a steady current, so the chips run warm and the cells are large.`,history:`IBM developed its first monolithic memory chip, the 16-bit SP95, in 1965 for the System/360 Model 95, a fast machine built for NASA. IBM's System/360 Model 85, announced in 1968, put a small fast buffer of 16 to 32 kilobytes in front of its core memory, the first commercial cache. Intel's first product, the 3101 of April 1969, was a 64-bit bipolar static RAM using fast Schottky transistors. The Cray-1 supercomputer of 1976 built its whole main memory from 1-kilobit bipolar chips for speed.`,facts:[`Intel's 3101 of 1969 stored 64 bits on one chip.`,`The Cray-1 of 1976 held up to 8 megabytes in bipolar memory chips.`]},"tech:dramcell":{intro:`The one-transistor DRAM cell stores a bit as a small electric charge on a capacitor, with a single MOS transistor acting as the door that lets charge in or out. Because the charge slowly leaks away, every cell must be read and topped up regularly. In return it is far smaller than a flip-flop of six transistors, so many more bits fit on a chip.`,how:`The transistor's gate connects to a word line that selects a row of cells; its other side connects to a bit line shared by a column. Opening a row lets each capacitor share its tiny charge with its bit line, and a sensitive amplifier decides whether the voltage nudged up or down, then writes the value back, because reading destroys it. Refreshing simply reads every row in turn every few milliseconds.`,history:`Robert Dennard at IBM's research centre in Yorktown Heights, New York, thought of the one-transistor cell in 1966 after seeing a talk on IBM's six-transistor MOS memory. He filed for a patent in 1967, and it was granted in June 1968. Intel's 1103 of 1970, the first commercially successful DRAM, used a three-transistor cell instead, because the single-transistor design needed more sensitive amplifiers. From the 4-kilobit generation of 1973 onwards, nearly every DRAM used Dennard's one-transistor cell. Dennard received the US National Medal of Technology in 1988 for the invention.`,facts:[`Dennard's DRAM patent, US number 3,387,286, was granted on 4 June 1968.`,`Dennard's 1966 cell needed one transistor where a static memory cell needed six.`]},"tech:dram1k":{intro:`The 1-kilobit DRAM is a single memory chip holding 1,024 bits as stored charges, each kept alive by regular refreshing. It is the first semiconductor memory cheap enough per bit to compete with magnetic core, which must be threaded by hand. A board of these chips replaces a stack of core planes holding thousands of tiny rings.`,how:`Each cell uses three transistors, one of which stores the bit as charge on its gate, built in Intel's p-channel silicon-gate process. The chip needs several supply voltages and careful timing signals, and the circuits around it must refresh every row about every two milliseconds. Chips are tested, sealed in plastic or ceramic packages and soldered onto memory boards in large arrays.`,history:`Intel introduced the 1103 in October 1970, developed from a design by Honeywell, which wanted cheaper memory for its computers. Microsystems International of Canada made the 1103 under licence from 1971 as a second source. By 1972 the 1103 was the best-selling semiconductor chip in the world, and it had made Intel profitable. Memory boards of 1103 chips began replacing core in minicomputers and mainframes in 1972 and 1973.`,facts:[`The Intel 1103 of 1970 used three transistors for each bit.`,`The 1103 of 1970 took about 300 nanoseconds to read a bit.`]},"tech:floppy":{intro:`A floppy disk is a thin, flexible plastic disc coated with magnetic oxide and sealed in a protective square sleeve, which a drive spins and reads through a slot. It is cheap, light and removable, so data and programs can be loaded, carried around and posted in an envelope. It holds far less than a hard disk and is slower, but costs a tiny fraction as much.`,how:`The disc turns inside its jacket, wiped clean by a soft lining, while a head touches the surface through an opening. The surface is divided into rings called tracks and segments called sectors, marked either by holes punched in the disc or by the drive's own formatting. A stepper motor moves the head from track to track. Because the head rubs the disc, both wear, and a disc left near a magnet or bent can lose its data.`,history:`David Noble's team at IBM in San Jose designed the 8-inch floppy disk in 1971 to load control programs, called microcode, into its System/370 mainframes. Alan Shugart, who had led disk work at IBM, founded Shugart Associates, which launched the 5¼-inch SA-400 minifloppy drive in 1976. Sony introduced the 3½-inch disk, in a rigid plastic case with a sliding metal shutter, in 1981. Apple's Macintosh of 1984 used Sony's 3½-inch drive. The high-density 3½-inch disk, holding 1.44 megabytes, arrived with IBM's PS/2 computers in 1987.`,facts:[`IBM's first 8-inch floppy of 1971 held about 80 kilobytes.`,`Shugart's SA-400 drive of 1976 sold for about $390.`,`The high-density 3½-inch disk of 1987 stored 1.44 megabytes.`]},"tech:eprom":{intro:`An EPROM, erasable programmable read-only memory, is a memory chip that keeps its contents with the power off, can be written electrically by the user, and can be wiped clean with strong ultraviolet light and used again. It lets engineers change the program inside a product while they develop it, instead of ordering a new factory-made chip each time.`,how:`Each cell is a MOS transistor with an extra 'floating' gate, completely wrapped in glass, between its control gate and the channel. A high programming voltage drives fast electrons onto the floating gate, where they stay trapped for years and stop the transistor turning on. The chip sits under a quartz window, and twenty minutes or so under an ultraviolet lamp gives the trapped electrons enough energy to escape, erasing every bit at once.`,history:`Dov Frohman, an engineer at Intel, invented the floating-gate EPROM in 1970 while studying faults in other chips. Intel introduced the 1702, holding 2,048 bits, in 1971. Intel's 16-kilobit 2716 of 1977 ran on a single 5-volt supply and became a standard part of early microcomputers. Until the mid-1980s EPROMs were among Intel's most profitable products, holding the programs of countless microprocessor-based products.`,facts:[`The Intel 1702 of 1971 stored 2,048 bits, or 256 bytes.`,`A 1970s EPROM was usually erased by about 20 minutes under a strong ultraviolet lamp.`]},"tech:winchester":{intro:`A Winchester disk seals its platters and heads together in a clean, closed box, and lets the heads land on the platter when it stops instead of pulling them away. Very light heads ride on a thin cushion of air dragged along by the spinning disk, much closer to the surface than older designs, so bits can be packed far tighter. The sealed unit is cheap to build and reliable.`,how:`Each head sits on a tiny slider shaped like a ski, pressed gently towards the platter by a spring. As the disk spins, air rushing under the slider lifts it a fraction of a micrometre. When the disk stops, the slider lands on a lubricated zone near the hub. Because dust never gets inside, the head can fly lower, and lower flying means smaller magnetic patches and more data on every platter.`,history:`Kenneth Haughton's team at IBM in San Jose developed the 3340 disk, launched in March 1973 with removable sealed modules each holding platters and heads. It was nicknamed Winchester in 1973 because the planned version had two 30-megabyte spindles, a '30-30' like the Winchester rifle. Seagate, co-founded by Alan Shugart in 1979, shipped the ST-506 in 1980, the first 5¼-inch hard disk. Rodime of Scotland introduced the first 3½-inch hard disk in 1983. IBM's PC XT of 1983 came with a 10-megabyte hard disk, bringing Winchester drives into ordinary offices.`,facts:[`Each IBM 3340 data module of 1973 held 35 or 70 megabytes.`,`The IBM PC XT of 1983 shipped with a 10 MB hard disk.`]},"tech:dram16k":{intro:`The 16-kilobit DRAM stores 16,384 bits on one chip using one-transistor cells, and fits in a small 16-pin package by sending each address in two halves down the same pins. It is the memory chip that makes thousands of bytes affordable in small computers, and its standard pin layout lets many makers' chips plug into the same boards.`,how:`A 16-kilobit chip needs 14 address bits. Instead of 14 pins it has 7: first the row address is put on them and latched by a 'row address strobe', then the column address with a 'column address strobe'. Opening a row moves 128 bits to sense amplifiers at once, and the column address picks one of them. Fewer pins mean a smaller, cheaper package and more chips on a board.`,history:`Mostek, in Carrollton, Texas, introduced address multiplexing with its 4-kilobit MK4096 in 1973, while rivals used larger 22-pin packages. Its MK4116 of 1976 carried the idea to 16 kilobits and became the most popular memory chip of the late 1970s. Japanese makers including NEC, Hitachi and Fujitsu made compatible 16-kilobit chips and won a large share of the American market by 1979. The original IBM PC of 1981 used 16-kilobit chips for its base memory. Mostek was bought by United Technologies in 1979 and sold to Thomson of France in 1985 after losing out to Japanese competition.`,facts:[`The MK4116 of 1976 needed three supplies: +12, +5 and -5 volts.`,`United Technologies paid about $345 million for Mostek in 1979.`]},"tech:eeprom":{intro:`EEPROM, electrically erasable programmable read-only memory, is memory that keeps its contents without power and can be erased and rewritten by an electrical signal, one byte at a time, while the chip stays in its circuit. No ultraviolet lamp or quartz window is needed. It suits small amounts of data that must change but survive power cuts, such as settings, calibrations and serial numbers.`,how:`As in an EPROM, each bit is held as charge on a floating gate, but the glass beneath part of the gate is made extremely thin, about ten nanometres. A strong electric field pushes electrons through it by quantum tunnelling to write, and pulls them back to erase. A second transistor in each cell lets single bytes be selected, which makes cells larger and dearer than EPROM cells. The thin oxide wears out after many thousands of rewrites.`,history:`Eli Harari at Hughes Aircraft developed a floating-gate memory erased by tunnelling through thin oxide in 1977. George Perlegos and his team at Intel produced the 2816, a 16-kilobit EEPROM, in 1980. Perlegos co-founded Seeq Technology in 1981 and Atmel in 1984, both makers of EEPROMs. Harari co-founded SunDisk, a maker of flash memory renamed SanDisk, in 1988. By the late 1980s EEPROMs held settings in televisions, car radios and telephones.`,facts:[`Intel's 2816 of 1980 stored 16 kilobits, or 2 kilobytes.`,`Typical EEPROMs of the 1980s were rated for about 10,000 rewrites of each byte.`]},"tech:dram256k":{intro:`The 256-kilobit DRAM stores a quarter of a million bits, 32 kilobytes, on one chip, sixteen times the 16-kilobit generation by way of 64 kilobits. Eight or nine chips make a quarter of a megabyte, so personal computers with half a megabyte or more of memory become affordable. Fierce competition makes it the cheapest memory yet.`,how:`The cells use one transistor and a flat capacitor, and like the 64-kilobit chips before it the chip runs from a single 5-volt supply, unlike the three needed earlier. Spare rows and columns are built in, and faulty ones are swapped out after testing by blowing tiny fuses with a laser, rescuing chips that would otherwise be scrap. Nine multiplexed address pins reach every bit.`,history:`Japanese makers, led by Fujitsu, Hitachi, NEC and Toshiba, took most of the world market for 64-kilobit DRAMs in 1981 and 1982 by investing heavily in new factories. Japanese makers began volume production of 256-kilobit chips by 1984 as prices tumbled. Apple's Macintosh 512K of September 1984 used sixteen 256-kilobit chips. A price collapse in 1985 left American makers losing money, and Intel announced in October 1985 that it was leaving the DRAM business. In 1986 the US and Japan signed a semiconductor trade agreement that set minimum prices for Japanese memory chips.`,facts:[`The Macintosh 512K of 1984 used sixteen 256 Kb chips for its 512 KB of memory.`,`Intel, which sold the first successful DRAM, quit the business in 1985.`]},"tech:cdrom":{intro:`An optical disc stores data as a spiral of microscopic pits pressed into a plastic disc, read by a laser beam that never touches the surface. A compact disc, 12 centimetres across, holds as much as several hundred floppy disks and can be stamped out in a factory for very little. It is read-only: ideal for publishing large programs, reference works and games.`,how:`A laser diode shines infrared light onto the spiral through the clear plastic. Flat areas reflect strongly, but at the edge of a pit the reflection drops, and a photodiode reads the changes as bits. Strong error-correcting codes, spread across the track, let the drive recover data despite scratches and dust. Discs are moulded against a metal master, then coated with aluminium and lacquer. The track is over five kilometres long.`,history:`Philips and Sony agreed the compact disc audio standard in 1980, and the first CD players went on sale in Japan in October 1982. The two companies extended the format to computer data as CD-ROM, and the first drives and discs appeared in 1985. Microsoft Encarta and the game Myst, both of 1993, helped make CD-ROM drives standard in home computers by the mid-1990s. The DVD, launched in Japan in 1996, used a shorter red laser and finer pits to hold 4.7 gigabytes on one layer.`,facts:[`A music CD of 1982 spins at about 500 rpm near the centre, slowing to about 200 at the edge.`,`The pits on a CD of 1982 are about half a micrometre wide.`,`A single-layer DVD of 1996 held 4.7 GB, about seven CDs' worth.`]},"tech:norflash":{intro:`NOR flash is memory that keeps its contents without power and is erased in large blocks at once rather than byte by byte. Giving up byte-by-byte erasing lets each cell shrink to a single transistor with a floating gate, so it is much denser and cheaper than EEPROM, yet any byte can be read quickly at random. That suits programs that run straight from the chip.`,how:`Cells are wired in parallel to their bit lines, as in a NOR logic gate, so any one can be read directly in tens of nanoseconds. Writing uses hot electrons, as in an EPROM, to charge a cell's floating gate. Erasing pulls the electrons off a whole block at once by tunnelling, in a fraction of a second. It suits firmware such as a PC's start-up code, a modem's software or a phone's operating system.`,history:`Fujio Masuoka at Toshiba invented flash memory and presented it at the International Electron Devices Meeting in 1984. Toshiba did little with it at first, and Intel, which saw a market for replacing EPROMs, launched a 256-kilobit NOR flash chip in 1988. Through the 1990s NOR flash held the start-up code of PCs and the software of mobile phones and modems. In 1993 AMD and Fujitsu formed a joint venture to make NOR flash, which took the name Spansion in the mid-2000s.`,facts:[`A NOR flash cell of 1988 used one transistor, against two for a byte-erasable EEPROM cell.`,`Masuoka first presented flash memory in 1984.`]},"tech:nandflash":{intro:`NAND flash is flash memory in which cells are joined in series, in strings of eight or sixteen, sharing contacts at the ends instead of each having its own. Fewer contacts make each cell much smaller, so NAND stores more bits in the same silicon and costs less per bit than NOR flash. It cannot read single bytes quickly, but it streams large blocks well.`,how:`To read one cell, every other transistor in its string is switched fully on, and the current through the string shows whether the chosen cell's floating gate holds charge. Data is read and written in pages of hundreds or thousands of bytes and erased in larger blocks, all by tunnelling through thin oxide. Some cells are faulty from the start or wear out, so a controller keeps track of them, corrects errors and spreads writes evenly. It suits files: photos, music and documents.`,history:`Fujio Masuoka's team at Toshiba proposed NAND flash in 1987, and Toshiba produced a 4-megabit NAND chip in 1989. SmartMedia cards, backed by Toshiba, appeared in 1995 and stored photos in early digital cameras. Apple's iPod nano of September 2005 replaced the hard disk of earlier iPods with NAND flash, and Apple bought a large share of the world's supply. Toshiba sold its memory business to a group led by Bain Capital in 2018, and it was renamed Kioxia in 2019.`,facts:[`The first iPod nano of 2005 held 2 or 4 GB of NAND flash.`,`Toshiba proposed the NAND design in 1987.`]},"tech:dram4m":{intro:`The 4-megabit DRAM holds about four million bits, half a megabyte, on a single chip, reached by way of the 1-megabit generation. Its cells are too small for a flat capacitor to hold enough charge to be read reliably, so the capacitor must grow downwards or upwards instead of sideways. Two rival designs, trenches and stacks, split the industry.`,how:`A DRAM capacitor needs roughly the same charge whatever the cell size, or the sense amplifier cannot tell a one from noise. A trench capacitor is a narrow hole etched several micrometres into the silicon and lined with insulating film, gaining area from its walls. A stacked capacitor is built above the transistor from folded layers of polysilicon, like fins or a chimney. Trenches keep the surface flat; stacks keep the capacitor away from the delicate transistor. Both add many process steps.`,history:`IBM, Toshiba and Siemens backed trench capacitors, while Hitachi, Samsung and others backed stacks, and both kinds of 4-megabit chip went on sale in 1989 and 1990. Samsung's choice of stacked cells, made in 1988, helped it catch up with Japanese makers. By the late 1990s most DRAM makers had settled on stacked capacitors. Qimonda, the memory arm of Infineon and the last big maker of trench DRAM, went bankrupt in January 2009.`,facts:[`A 4 Mb DRAM of 1990 stored 512 kilobytes on one chip.`,`Trench capacitors of 1990 were etched several micrometres deep into the silicon.`]},"tech:sdram":{intro:`Synchronous DRAM is memory that works in step with a clock signal from the system, instead of responding whenever its control signals change. Because the chip knows exactly when each step happens, it can accept a new request while finishing the last and send out a burst of data on successive clock ticks. Memory can then keep up with fast processors far better than older chips.`,how:`The memory controller sends commands, such as 'open this row' or 'read from this column', on clock edges. The chip is split into two or four banks that can work on different rows at once, so one bank can be opened while another delivers data. After a set number of clock cycles, the latency, data flows out word after word in a burst. Settings in a mode register tell the chip how long its bursts and latency should be.`,history:`Samsung introduced the KM48SL2000, a 16-megabit synchronous DRAM, in 1992. JEDEC, the electronics industry's standards body, adopted the SDRAM standard in 1993. In 1995 most PCs ran on asynchronous EDO memory, which struggled to keep up with faster processor buses. SDRAM modules took over in PCs in 1996 and 1997 as chipsets began to support them. Intel's PC100 specification of 1998 set the standard for 100 MHz memory modules, and PC133 followed in 1999.`,facts:[`PC100 SDRAM of 1998 moved 800 MB a second on a 64-bit module.`,`Samsung's first SDRAM of 1992 held 16 megabits.`]},"tech:dram64m":{intro:`The 64-megabit DRAM holds 64 million bits, eight megabytes, on a single chip, reached by way of the 16-megabit generation. Eight of them on a module give a personal computer 64 megabytes of memory. Its cells are made in processes of about 350 nanometres and below, with capacitors built tall and wrinkled to hold enough charge.`,how:`Stacked capacitors are shaped like crowns or cylinders, and some have deliberately roughened silicon surfaces, called hemispherical grain, that multiply their area. The chips run from 3.3 volts and come as synchronous DRAM. Each new density takes years of development, and companies race to reach volume production first, because the first maker can charge high prices before rivals catch up and prices fall.`,history:`Samsung developed one of the first 64-megabit DRAMs in 1992, nine years after starting its memory business in 1983. Samsung became the world's largest memory chip maker in 1993. Prices of memory chips collapsed in 1996 as new factories came on line. Sixty-four-megabit chips became the mainstream density in 1997 and 1998. The Asian financial crisis of 1997 and 1998 hit Korean makers hard, and Hyundai bought LG Semicon in 1999, forming the company renamed Hynix in 2001.`,facts:[`Samsung made its first DRAMs, 64-kilobit chips, in 1983.`,`Hyundai's purchase of LG Semicon in 1999 merged Korea's second and third memory makers.`]},"tech:gmr":{intro:`A GMR read head senses the bits on a hard disk using giant magnetoresistance: in a sandwich of magnetic and non-magnetic metal films only a few atoms thick, the electrical resistance changes sharply depending on whether the magnetic layers point the same way or opposite ways. Even the weak field of a tiny bit can swing one layer, so the head reads bits far smaller than older heads could.`,how:`One magnetic layer is pinned in a fixed direction; the other, separated from it by a thin film of copper, swings freely with the field from the disk passing beneath. Electrons pass through the sandwich more easily when the two layers are aligned, because their spins match, and less easily when they are opposed. A small current through the head turns the passing bits into a changing voltage. The head only reads; a separate element writes.`,history:`Albert Fert in Paris and Peter Grünberg in Jülich, Germany, independently discovered giant magnetoresistance in 1988. Stuart Parkin at IBM's Almaden laboratory in San Jose showed in 1990 that the effect could be made with ordinary sputtered films, opening the way to mass production. IBM shipped the first GMR heads in its Deskstar 16GP drive at the end of 1997. From 1997 to about 2003 the amount of data packed into each square inch of disk roughly doubled every year. Fert and Grünberg shared the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2007.`,facts:[`IBM's Deskstar 16GP of 1997 stored 16.8 GB.`,`IBM's first magnetoresistive heads, of an older and weaker type, shipped in 1991.`]},"tech:ddr":{intro:`DDR SDRAM, double data rate synchronous DRAM, moves data on both the rising and the falling edge of each clock tick, so it carries twice as much per cycle as ordinary SDRAM without the clock running any faster. It helps memory feed processors whose speed has raced ahead, and it runs on a lower 2.5-volt supply.`,how:`Inside, the chip fetches two words at once from its arrays, a '2n prefetch', and sends them out one on each clock edge. To time data that precisely, a strobe signal travels alongside the data, and a delay-locked loop lines the chip's output up with the clock. Signals use a terminated interface that swings less voltage and copes with fast edges. Modules have 184 pins and a notch in a different place from older SDRAM, so the two cannot be mixed.`,history:`Samsung showed one of the first DDR SDRAM chips, of 64 megabits, in 1998. JEDEC published the DDR standard in 2000. AMD's 760 chipset of late 2000 brought DDR memory to Athlon PCs, while Intel backed the rival Rambus memory for its Pentium 4. Rambus memory proved expensive, and in early 2002 Intel's 845 chipset added DDR support for the Pentium 4. By 2003 DDR was the standard memory of new PCs.`,facts:[`DDR ran at 2.5 volts, against 3.3 volts for SDRAM (2000).`,`PC3200 DDR modules of 2003 moved 3.2 GB a second.`]},"tech:mlc":{intro:`Multi-level cell flash stores two bits in each memory cell instead of one, by placing four distinct amounts of charge on its floating gate instead of just two. Doubling the bits per cell nearly halves the cost of each gigabyte without shrinking the cells at all. The price is slower writing, more errors and a shorter working life.`,how:`Writing creeps up on the target charge in small pulses, checking after each, until the cell's threshold voltage falls inside one of four narrow windows. Reading compares the cell against three reference levels rather than one. Because the windows sit close together, a little leakage or wear can shift a cell into the wrong one, so controllers add stronger error-correcting codes. A two-bit cell survives far fewer erase cycles than a one-bit cell.`,history:`Intel's StrataFlash of 1997 was the first multi-level flash, storing two bits in each NOR cell. Samsung and Toshiba both made 1-gigabit two-bit NAND chips in 2001, aimed at memory cards. By the mid-2000s most NAND flash sold stored two bits per cell. Three-bit cells reached consumer solid-state drives in Samsung's 840 in 2012, and four-bit cells followed in 2018.`,facts:[`A two-bit cell needs four charge levels, a three-bit cell eight (2001).`,`Samsung's 840 SSD of 2012 stored three bits in each cell.`]},"tech:gddr":{intro:`Graphics DRAM is memory designed for graphics cards, where a processor drawing millions of pixels needs a flood of data more than cheap capacity. GDDR chips are soldered close around the graphics processor on short, carefully matched traces, which lets them run at much higher data rates than the memory sticks of a PC. They cost more per bit and use more power, but deliver several times the bandwidth.`,how:`Each chip has a wide 32-bit interface, and a card uses eight or more chips side by side, giving a bus 256 or more bits wide. Short point-to-point wires and termination built into the chips let signals switch very fast without reflections. GDDR3 is built on DDR2 technology tuned for speed and heat. Graphics work streams through memory in big, predictable chunks, which suits long, fast bursts.`,history:`Graphics cards of the 1990s used special memories such as VRAM, which had an extra port for feeding the display. ATI drew up the GDDR3 design with JEDEC, and graphics cards using it went on sale in 2004. Microsoft's Xbox 360 of 2005 used 512 megabytes of GDDR3 as its only main memory. AMD's Radeon HD 4870 of June 2008 was the first card with GDDR5, which sends four transfers per clock. GDDR6 arrived on NVIDIA's RTX 20 cards in 2018.`,facts:[`The Radeon HD 4870 of 2008 moved 115 GB a second over a 256-bit bus.`,`The Xbox 360 of 2005 shared 512 MB of GDDR3 between processor and graphics.`]},"tech:dram1g":{intro:`The 1-gigabit DRAM holds a billion bits, 128 megabytes, on one chip, reached by way of 256 and 512 megabits. Eight of them make a gigabyte memory module, enough for a PC to keep several large programs open at once. Its cells are made at 90 nanometres and finer, with capacitors many times taller than they are wide.`,how:`Each cell takes under a tenth of a square micrometre. Capacitor walls use insulators with high dielectric constants, such as aluminium or hafnium oxide, so a thin film holds more charge. Some cells use 'recessed' transistors whose channel dips into the silicon, keeping it long enough to stop charge leaking away while the cell's footprint shrinks. The chips come in DDR2 form, running on 1.8 volts.`,history:`JEDEC published the DDR2 standard in 2003, and DDR2 memory came to PCs with Intel's 915 chipset in June 2004. Samsung began mass production of 1-gigabit DDR2 chips at 90 nanometres in 2005. Microsoft's Windows Vista, launched in January 2007, asked for at least a gigabyte of memory for its full Aero look. In 2007 1-gigabit chips became the most-shipped DRAM density, and gigabyte modules became common in new PCs.`,facts:[`DDR2 ran at 1.8 volts, against 2.5 for DDR (2004).`,`A 1 Gb chip of 2005 held 1,073,741,824 bits.`]},"tech:perpendicular":{intro:`Perpendicular recording stores the bits on a hard disk as tiny magnets standing upright, pointing up or down through the coating, instead of lying flat along the track. Upright magnets can be packed closer together without weakening one another, and the coating can be thicker and more stable. It lets disk makers keep raising capacity when the old method was running out of room.`,how:`In the old longitudinal method, very small bits lying end to end push on each other and can flip at random from heat, a barrier called the superparamagnetic limit. Perpendicular disks add a soft magnetic underlayer beneath the recording film; the write head's field passes straight down through the bit into the underlayer and back up through a wide return pole. That stronger field can write coatings that hold their magnetism more firmly.`,history:`Shun-ichi Iwasaki at Tohoku University in Japan proposed perpendicular magnetic recording in the mid-1970s. Toshiba shipped the first perpendicular hard disks in 2005, 1.8-inch drives of 40 and 80 gigabytes for music players. Seagate followed with laptop and desktop drives in 2006. Hitachi, which had bought IBM's disk business in 2003, launched the first 1-terabyte desktop drive, the Deskstar 7K1000, in 2007.`,facts:[`Hitachi's Deskstar 7K1000 of 2007 held 1 TB on five platters.`,`Toshiba's first perpendicular drives of 2005 were 1.8 inches across.`]},"tech:ddr3":{intro:`DDR3 is the third generation of double data rate memory. Each read fetches eight words from inside the chip and sends them out in one fast burst, so the outside connection can run at twice the speed of DDR2 while the memory arrays inside stay just as slow. It runs on 1.5 volts, saving power in laptops and servers.`,how:`DRAM arrays cannot get much faster, so each generation widens the internal 'prefetch' instead: two words for DDR, four for DDR2 and eight for DDR3. Modules route the command and address wires past each chip in turn, a 'fly-by' layout, and the controller measures the delay to each chip during start-up, a step called write levelling. Termination inside the chips and calibrated drivers keep signals clean at over a billion transfers a second.`,history:`JEDEC published the DDR3 standard in 2007. Intel's P35 chipset of June 2007 was the first to support DDR3 in PCs. Intel's Core i7 of November 2008 used DDR3 only, with three memory channels built into the processor. A low-voltage version, DDR3L, running on 1.35 volts, was standardised in 2010. DDR4, on 1.2 volts, began replacing DDR3 in new PCs in 2014 and 2015.`,facts:[`DDR3-1600 modules of about 2010 moved 12.8 GB a second.`,`The Core i7 of 2008 reached about 25.6 GB a second over three DDR3 channels.`]},"tech:ssd":{intro:`A solid-state drive stores files on flash memory chips instead of spinning platters, behind a controller that makes it look like an ordinary disk to the computer. With no moving parts it finds any piece of data almost instantly, uses less power, makes no noise and survives knocks. It costs far more per gigabyte than a hard disk.`,how:`Flash must be erased in large blocks before it can be rewritten, and each block wears out after a few thousand erasures. The controller, a small processor, hides this with a 'flash translation layer': it writes new data to fresh pages, keeps a map of where everything really is, tidies up blocks in the background and spreads wear evenly. It reads from many flash chips in parallel for speed. Spare capacity, error correction and a DRAM cache keep performance steady.`,history:`SunDisk, co-founded by Eli Harari, shipped a 20-megabyte flash drive in 1991. Through the 1990s flash drives were used mainly in rugged military and industrial computers, where their price mattered less. Apple's MacBook Air of January 2008 offered a 64-gigabyte SSD as an expensive option. Intel's X25-M of September 2008 used a controller that kept small random reads and writes fast, setting the standard for PC drives. NVMe, a standard that connected SSDs directly to the PCI Express bus, was published in 2011.`,facts:[`Intel's X25-M of 2008 held 80 GB and cost about $595.`,`In 2008 a hard disk took about 10 ms to reach a random block; an SSD under 0.1 ms.`]},"tech:dram4g":{intro:`The 4-gigabit DRAM holds four billion bits, half a gigabyte, on one chip, made with features around 30 nanometres. Sixteen of them make an 8-gigabyte memory module. Each capacitor is a hollow cylinder dozens of times taller than its width, with walls coated in an insulating film only a few nanometres thick.`,how:`Shrinking DRAM is harder than shrinking logic: each smaller cell must store about as much charge as before and keep it for the 64 milliseconds between refreshes, while the transistor guarding it must leak almost nothing. Films of zirconium oxide layered with aluminium oxide hold more charge in a thin coat. Transistors are buried in trenches under the word lines, with their gates inside the silicon, to lengthen their channels. Arrays are printed with double patterning.`,history:`Samsung began volume production of 30-nanometre-class DDR3 in 2010 and brought 4-gigabit chips on that process to market in 2011. Elpida, Japan's last DRAM maker, went bankrupt in February 2012 and was taken over by Micron in 2013. DDR4 chips of 4 gigabits arrived with the first DDR4 computers in 2014. By 2015 Samsung, SK Hynix and Micron made almost all the world's DRAM.`,facts:[`A DRAM cell of 2010 had to hold its charge for 64 milliseconds between refreshes.`,`Elpida filed for bankruptcy in 2012 with debts of about 448 billion yen.`]},"tech:xtorcomputer":{intro:`A transistor computer replaces the thousands of vacuum tubes of earlier machines with transistors: small, cool, long-lived switches. Tubes burn out, need large power supplies and fill rooms with heat; transistors use a fraction of the power and rarely fail. The computer becomes smaller, more reliable and cheaper to run, though early transistors are costly and slow.`,how:`Logic is built from transistors, diodes and resistors soldered onto small printed cards that plug into a frame, each card holding a gate, a flip-flop or a few bits. Hundreds of identical cards make up the machine, so a faulty one can be swapped in seconds. Memory is magnetic core, with drums, disks or tape for bulk data. Because transistors need only a few volts, the power supplies and air-conditioning shrink dramatically.`,history:`Tom Kilburn's team at the University of Manchester ran a prototype transistor computer in November 1953, using point-contact transistors. Bell Labs completed TRADIC for the US Air Force in 1954, with about 700 transistors, drawing less than 100 watts. IBM's 7090 of 1959, a transistor version of its 709 tube machine, was several times faster. IBM's 1401 of 1959, a cheaper transistor business computer, sold more than 10,000 units and brought many firms their first computer.`,facts:[`Bell Labs' TRADIC of 1954 used about 700 transistors and over 10,000 germanium diodes.`,`IBM's 1401 of 1959 rented from about $2,500 a month.`]},"tech:ttl":{intro:`Standard logic is a family of small integrated circuits, each holding a few gates, flip-flops or counters, that all share one supply voltage and the same signal levels, so any one can drive any other. TTL, transistor-transistor logic, is its most popular form. Engineers build a computer or controller by choosing parts from a catalogue and wiring them together on circuit boards.`,how:`A TTL gate uses a transistor with several emitters as its input, one for each signal, followed by an output stage that actively pulls high or low, which makes it fast and able to drive many other inputs. Everything runs on 5 volts, with anything below about 0.8 volts read as zero and above 2 volts as one. Chips come in dual in-line packages of 14 or 16 pins, and a numbered part, such as a quad two-input NAND gate, behaves the same whoever makes it.`,history:`James Buie at TRW invented transistor-transistor logic in 1961. Sylvania brought out the first TTL family, called SUHL, in 1963 for military and aerospace use. Texas Instruments launched its 5400 series for the military in 1964 and the cheaper 7400 series in plastic packages in 1966. The 7400 series was copied by dozens of makers, and minicomputers such as Data General's Nova of 1969 were built from it.`,facts:[`A 7400 chip of 1966 held four two-input NAND gates.`,`Standard TTL gates of 1966 switched in about 10 nanoseconds.`]},"tech:compatfamily":{intro:`A compatible computer family is a range of computers, from small and cheap to large and fast, that all run exactly the same programs and use the same disks, tapes and printers. A customer can start small and move up to a bigger model without rewriting software or replacing equipment. It separates the instructions programs depend on from the circuits that carry them out.`,how:`Every model obeys one written architecture: the instructions, registers and data formats a programmer sees. Inside, cheaper models carry out each instruction slowly, step by step, using microcode, a tiny program in fast read-only memory, while expensive ones use fast hard-wired circuits. Standard interfaces let the same peripherals plug into any model. Smaller models can even imitate older machines, so customers can bring their existing programs along.`,history:`IBM's SPREAD committee recommended a single compatible line of computers in December 1961, to replace IBM's several incompatible ranges. IBM announced System/360 on 7 April 1964 with six models and some 150 new products. Gene Amdahl was its chief architect, and Fred Brooks managed the hardware and then the troubled OS/360 software in the mid-1960s. System/360 made the 8-bit byte the standard unit of data in 1964. Its successor, System/370 of 1970, kept full compatibility with it.`,facts:[`IBM spent about $5 billion on System/360, more than its revenue for 1964.`,`IBM's revenue grew from about $3.2 billion in 1964 to $7.5 billion in 1970.`]},"tech:supercomputer":{intro:`A supercomputer is a machine built purely for speed at scientific calculation, for jobs such as weather forecasting, nuclear weapon design and aircraft simulation, where faster answers are worth almost any price. Its designers use the fastest circuits available, keep wires short, run many units at once and work hard at removing the heat that results.`,how:`Instead of one unit doing everything, the machine has several independent arithmetic units, for adding, multiplying and so on, which work at the same time while small separate processors handle input and output. Instructions are overlapped so a new one starts before the last has finished. Circuit boards are packed tightly to shorten wires, since a signal travels only about 30 centimetres in a nanosecond, and refrigerant flows through cold plates to carry heat away.`,history:`Seymour Cray designed the CDC 6600 at Control Data's laboratory in Chippewa Falls, Wisconsin, and the first was delivered in 1964. The 6600 of 1964 was about three times faster than IBM's Stretch, the previous fastest computer. In August 1963 IBM's chief, Thomas Watson Jr, had sent a memo asking how a laboratory of 34 people, counting the janitor, had beaten IBM. Cray left Control Data to found Cray Research in 1972. The first Cray-1, with its C-shaped cabinet and vector processing, was installed at Los Alamos in 1976.`,facts:[`The CDC 6600 of 1964 had 10 functional units and 10 peripheral processors.`,`A Cray-1 of 1976 cost about $8 million and weighed about 5.5 tonnes.`,`Control Data sold about 100 CDC 6600s in the 1960s.`]},"tech:minicomputer":{intro:`A minicomputer is a small, comparatively cheap computer that a single laboratory, factory or department can own, instead of renting time on a company mainframe. It fits in a cabinet or on a bench, needs no special air-conditioned room, and is used directly by engineers and scientists, often wired straight to instruments and machines.`,how:`Minicomputers use short words of 12 or 16 bits, a few thousand words of core memory and simple instructions, built from transistor cards or small integrated circuits. They are sold without much software or service to customers who program them themselves, and to companies that build them into their own products, such as typesetters, machine tools and laboratory equipment. A teleprinter serves as keyboard, printer and paper-tape reader.`,history:`Ken Olsen and Harlan Anderson founded Digital Equipment Corporation in an old woollen mill in Maynard, Massachusetts, in 1957. DEC's PDP-8, launched in March 1965, used 12-bit words and was small enough to sit on a laboratory bench. Data General, founded in 1968 by engineers who had left DEC, launched the 16-bit Nova in 1969. DEC's 16-bit PDP-11 of 1970 became the best-selling minicomputer family, and Unix was developed on it at Bell Labs in the early 1970s. DEC's VAX-11/780 of 1977 began the age of 32-bit 'superminis'.`,facts:[`The basic PDP-8 of 1965 had 4,096 words of 12-bit core memory.`,`DEC was founded in 1957 with $70,000 from American Research and Development.`]},"tech:calcchip":{intro:`A calculator on a chip puts all the logic of an electronic calculator, the arithmetic, keyboard scanning and display control, into a single MOS integrated circuit. Add a keypad, a numeric display and a battery, and the result fits in a pocket. Desk calculators built from dozens of chips can shrink into cheap handheld gadgets.`,how:`The chip works on numbers digit by digit in binary-coded decimal, with each decimal digit taking four bits, and does sums one digit at a time in a loop, which is slow but needs few transistors. A built-in read-only memory holds the sequence of steps for each key. The chip scans the keyboard rows and lights the display digits one after another so fast that they all seem lit at once. Low-power MOS lets it run for hours on batteries.`,history:`Texas Instruments built the Cal Tech prototype in 1967, a handheld calculator using a few chips designed by Jack Kilby's team. Busicom's Handy LE-120A of 1971, using Mostek's MK6010, was among the first pocket calculators built around a single calculator chip. Gary Boone at Texas Instruments designed the TMS1802NC single-chip calculator in 1971. Hewlett-Packard's HP-35 of 1972 was the first scientific pocket calculator, working out logarithms and sines at the press of a key. By the late 1970s a basic pocket calculator cost only a few pounds or dollars.`,facts:[`The HP-35 of 1972 was named for its 35 keys.`,`The Sinclair Executive of 1972 was under a centimetre thick.`]},"tech:microprocessor":{intro:`A microprocessor is a computer's central processing unit, the part that fetches instructions and does arithmetic and logic, made on a single chip. Instead of designing new circuits for every product, an engineer can use the same general-purpose chip and give it a different program stored in memory. With memory and input-output chips, it forms a small but complete computer.`,how:`The chip contains registers, an arithmetic and logic unit, a program counter and a control unit that decodes each instruction it fetches from memory. Data moves over a narrow bus a few bits at a time, while support chips hold the program in read-only memory, keep data in RAM and handle keys and displays. Programs are written by hand in assembly language, then burned into ROM.`,history:`Busicom, a Japanese calculator company, asked Intel in 1969 to make a set of chips for a range of calculators. Intel's Ted Hoff and Stanley Mazor proposed a single general-purpose processor instead, and Federico Faggin designed the chip with Busicom's Masatoshi Shima in 1970 and 1971. Intel bought back the rights to sell the chip for other uses, and launched the 4004 in November 1971. Intel's 8-bit 8008, first designed for the terminal maker Computer Terminal Corporation, followed in April 1972.`,facts:[`The Intel 4004 of 1971 held about 2,300 transistors and ran at up to 740 kHz.`,`The 4004's chip of 1971 measured about 3 by 4 millimetres.`]},"tech:mcu":{intro:`A microcontroller is a whole small computer on one chip: a processor plus its own program memory, data memory, timers and input-output pins. It needs almost nothing around it, so it can be built cheaply into toys, washing machines, car engines, keyboards and remote controls. It trades speed and size for low cost and simplicity.`,how:`The program is fixed in a mask ROM during manufacture, or held in EPROM for small batches, and the RAM holds only a few dozen to a few hundred bytes. Pins connect straight to switches, lights, motors and sensors, and built-in timers count time or make pulses. Because millions of identical chips are made for one product, every cent saved matters, so the processor is simple, often 4 or 8 bits wide.`,history:`Gary Boone and Michael Cochran at Texas Instruments designed the TMS1000 series, offered to customers in 1974. Milton Bradley's electronic game Simon of 1978 ran on a TMS1000. Intel's 8048 of 1976 and 8051 of 1980 brought microcontrollers to industry, and the 8048 went into the keyboard of the IBM PC in 1981. Motorola's 68HC11 of 1985 found its way into car engine controls and countless gadgets.`,facts:[`The Intel 8048 of 1976 had 1 KB of ROM and 64 bytes of RAM.`,`The Intel 8051 of 1980 had 4 KB of ROM and 128 bytes of RAM.`]},"tech:micro8":{intro:`Eight-bit microprocessors handle data a byte at a time and can address 64 kilobytes of memory, enough for a useful general-purpose computer. They are fast enough to run a programming language such as BASIC, a word processor or a video game, and cheap enough to build into a machine one person can afford. Most come with a small family of support chips.`,how:`A typical design has an 8-bit accumulator, a handful of other registers, a 16-bit address bus and around a hundred instructions, each taking a few clock cycles at one to four megahertz. Some, like the Z80, pack in extra registers and refresh dynamic memory themselves; others, like the 6502, stay minimal and make up for it with clever ways of addressing memory. Programs are written in assembly language or in BASIC interpreted from ROM.`,history:`Intel's 8080 of April 1974 became the processor of the Altair 8800, a kit computer from the New Mexico firm MITS that appeared on the cover of Popular Electronics in January 1975. Chuck Peddle and a team who had left Motorola designed the 6502 at MOS Technology, launched in September 1975. Federico Faggin, who had left Intel, founded Zilog and released the Z80 in July 1976. The Apple II and Commodore PET of 1977 used the 6502, and Tandy's TRS-80 of 1977 the Z80. Nintendo's Famicom of 1983 used a chip based on the 6502.`,facts:[`MITS sold the Altair 8800 kit for $397 in 1975.`,`The 6502 of 1975 had only about 3,500 transistors.`]},"tech:micro16":{intro:`Sixteen- and 32-bit microprocessors handle numbers two or four bytes at a time and can reach a megabyte of memory or more, instead of the 64 kilobytes of 8-bit chips. They bring the power of a minicomputer to a single chip, opening the way to business personal computers, engineering workstations and machines shared by several users.`,how:`Registers are 16 or 32 bits wide, multiplication and division are built in, and addresses grow to 20 bits or more. Intel's 8086 reaches a megabyte by combining two 16-bit values, a segment and an offset, so programs must juggle 64-kilobyte windows. Motorola's 68000 uses 32-bit registers throughout with a flat 24-bit address space of 16 megabytes. The chips hold tens of thousands of transistors and sell for a few hundred dollars.`,history:`Intel's 8086, designed by a small team including Stephen Morse, launched in June 1978, and its programs could be translated from the older 8080. Motorola's 68000 followed in 1979 with a cleaner 32-bit design. IBM chose the 8088, an 8086 with an 8-bit outside bus that made boards cheaper, for its Personal Computer of August 1981. Apple's Macintosh of 1984 and the Commodore Amiga and Atari ST of 1985 used the 68000. Intel's 386 of 1985 extended the 8086 family to 32 bits.`,facts:[`The 68000 of 1979 could address 16 MB of memory.`,`The IBM PC of 1981 ran its 8088 at 4.77 MHz.`]},"tech:vlsidesign":{intro:`VLSI design rules are a simple, systematic way of laying out very large-scale integrated chips, set out by Carver Mead and Lynn Conway. Instead of drawing each transistor by hand to a factory's secret rules, designers follow a few rules measured in one unit, lambda, that scales with the process. Computer scientists, not only circuit specialists, can then design whole chips.`,how:`Every width and spacing is a multiple of lambda, half the smallest feature: a polysilicon wire, for instance, must be at least two lambda wide. A layout drawn this way can be made in any factory that meets the rules, and shrunk simply by changing lambda. Designers build structured, repeating blocks, such as registers and data paths, and describe layouts with software. Multi-project wafers put dozens of student designs on one wafer, sharing the cost.`,history:`Lynn Conway taught the first course on the method at MIT in autumn 1978, and her students' designs were made on a shared wafer and returned within weeks. Mead and Conway's textbook, Introduction to VLSI Systems, was published in 1980 and was soon taught at universities across the US. In 1981 the MOSIS service, funded by the US defence research agency DARPA, began making chips for universities at the University of Southern California. Berkeley's RISC-I and Stanford's MIPS processors were designed this way in 1981 and 1982.`,facts:[`The MPC79 project of 1979 put 82 designs by 124 designers onto shared wafers.`,`MOSIS began making student and research chips in 1981.`]},"tech:dsp":{intro:`A digital signal processor is a microprocessor built for one kind of work: crunching streams of numbers that represent real-world signals, such as sound, speech, radio waves or images, as fast as they arrive. At its heart is a hardware multiplier that can multiply two numbers and add the result to a running total in a single clock cycle, the core step of filtering signals.`,how:`Signals are sampled thousands of times a second, and each sample is processed by sums of the form 'multiply by a coefficient and add', repeated dozens of times. A DSP has separate memories and buses for program and data, so it can fetch an instruction and two numbers at once, plus loops with no overhead and special addressing for buffers that wrap round. It usually works in whole-number arithmetic, which is cheaper than floating point, and does in software what racks of analogue filters once did.`,history:`Bell Labs built its DSP-1 in 1979 for the telephone network. NEC's µPD7720 appeared in 1980 as one of the first single-chip DSPs sold to other companies. Texas Instruments' TMS32010 of 1983 did a multiply in 200 nanoseconds and became the most popular early DSP. By the 1990s DSPs sat inside modems, hard disk drives and digital mobile phones, and Texas Instruments was the leading supplier.`,facts:[`The TMS32010 of 1983 ran 5 million instructions a second.`,`Compact disc audio, from 1982, is sampled 44,100 times a second.`]},"tech:risc":{intro:`RISC, reduced instruction set computing, is a way of designing processors around a small set of simple instructions that each do one thing and finish in about one clock cycle. Complex operations are built in software from these simple steps. The simpler chip can run faster, leaves room for useful hardware such as many registers and pipelining, and suits compilers better than processors with hundreds of elaborate instructions.`,how:`All instructions are the same length and format, so they can be decoded quickly. Only special load and store instructions touch memory; everything else works on a large set of registers inside the chip. Each instruction passes through a pipeline of stages, fetch, decode, execute, memory and write-back, so five are in progress at once like cars on an assembly line. Compilers are trusted to arrange instructions so the pipeline rarely waits.`,history:`John Cocke's team at IBM's research centre in Yorktown Heights began the 801 project in 1975, aiming at a fast, simple processor for telephone switching. David Patterson at the University of California, Berkeley, coined the term RISC, and his students built the RISC-I chip in 1981 and 1982. John Hennessy's MIPS project at Stanford began in 1981, and he co-founded MIPS Computer Systems in 1984. The MIPS R2000 went on sale in 1986 and Sun's SPARC in 1987. HP's PA-RISC of 1986 and IBM's POWER of 1990 followed.`,facts:[`Berkeley's RISC-I of 1982 had about 44,000 transistors.`,`John Cocke received the Turing Award in 1987.`,`Patterson and Hennessy shared the Turing Award for 2017.`]},"tech:lowpowercore":{intro:`A low-power RISC core is a processor design small and frugal enough to run on a battery, and simple enough that other companies can license it and build it into their own chips. Instead of selling chips, the designer sells the blueprint and collects a royalty on every chip made with it. Its small size leaves room for the licensee's own circuits on the same piece of silicon.`,how:`The design keeps RISC's fixed-length instructions and a short, simple pipeline, and leaves out anything costly in transistors, such as complex instructions. Almost every instruction can be made conditional, which saves branches. Fewer transistors switching means far less power and heat, so no fan or heatsink is needed. The core is delivered to licensees as a design they can place beside memories, radios and their own logic.`,history:`Sophie Wilson and Steve Furber at Acorn Computers in Cambridge designed the ARM1, which first ran in April 1985. In one test in 1985 the ARM1 kept working with its power supply disconnected, running on the tiny currents leaking in from its input signals. The ARM2 powered Acorn's Archimedes computers in 1987. Acorn, Apple and VLSI Technology formed Advanced RISC Machines as a separate company in November 1990. Its ARM610 ran Apple's Newton MessagePad in 1993, and the ARM7TDMI went into Nokia phone chips made by Texas Instruments in 1997.`,facts:[`The ARM2 of 1987 had about 30,000 transistors.`,`ARM began in 1990 with 12 engineers from Acorn.`]},"tech:eda":{intro:`Logic synthesis is software that turns a description of what a chip should do, written in a hardware description language, into a detailed list of logic gates and their connections. Engineers describe registers, arithmetic and control in lines of text instead of drawing thousands of gates by hand. The tool optimises the result for speed and area using a library of standard cells from the chip factory.`,how:`Designers write behaviour at the 'register transfer level' in languages such as Verilog or VHDL, then run simulations to check it. The synthesis tool translates the text into Boolean logic, simplifies it and maps it onto the cells a factory offers, such as NAND gates and flip-flops, trying alternatives until timing goals are met. Place-and-route tools then lay out the cells and wires automatically. One engineer can handle designs with hundreds of thousands of gates.`,history:`Phil Moorby and Prabhu Goel at Gateway Design Automation created the Verilog language for simulation in 1984. The US Department of Defense sponsored VHDL, which became an IEEE standard in 1987. Aart de Geus and colleagues from General Electric founded the company that became Synopsys in 1986, and its Design Compiler of 1987 produced gates from hardware descriptions. Cadence bought Gateway in 1989 and opened Verilog to the whole industry in 1990. By the mid-1990s nearly all large digital chips were designed with synthesis.`,facts:[`VHDL became IEEE standard 1076 in 1987.`,`Synopsys began in 1986 in North Carolina's Research Triangle Park.`]},"tech:onchipcache":{intro:`An on-chip cache keeps copies of recently used instructions and data in a small, fast memory on the processor chip itself, so most requests never have to go out to slower main memory. Putting a floating-point unit on the same chip means fractional and scientific maths no longer waits for a separate coprocessor. Together they let the processor do useful work on almost every clock tick.`,how:`A cache is organised in lines of 16 or more bytes; when the processor asks for an address, the cache checks a small directory of tags to see whether it holds that line. Programs tend to reuse the same data and walk through memory in order, so a cache of only 8 kilobytes can answer around nine requests in ten. A miss fetches a whole line from memory. The floating-point unit, formerly a separate chip in its own socket, shares the chip and its fast internal buses.`,history:`Motorola's 68020 of 1984 included a small 256-byte instruction cache. Intel's i486, announced in April 1989, put an 8-kilobyte cache and a floating-point unit on the chip beside a pipelined integer unit that did most simple instructions in one clock. Motorola's 68040 of 1990 did the same for its family. Intel's Pentium of 1993 split the cache into separate 8-kilobyte instruction and data caches. The Pentium Pro of 1995 placed a second-level cache in the same package, and in 1999 the Coppermine Pentium III moved it onto the chip itself.`,facts:[`The i486 of 1989 had one 8 KB cache shared by instructions and data.`,`Intel's 486SX of 1991 was a 486 with its floating-point unit disabled, sold cheaper.`]},"tech:ooo":{intro:`Out-of-order execution lets a processor run instructions as soon as their inputs are ready, instead of strictly in the order the program lists them. If one instruction is waiting for data from memory, independent ones behind it go ahead. A reorder buffer then puts the results back in the original order, so the program sees exactly what it would have seen otherwise, only sooner.`,how:`Instructions are fetched and decoded several at a time into a pool. Register renaming gives each result its own hidden register, so instructions that merely reuse a register's name need not wait for each other. Each instruction waits in a reservation station until its operands arrive, then goes to a free execution unit. Results retire in program order from the reorder buffer, and if a branch was guessed wrongly, the work after it is thrown away.`,history:`Seymour Cray's CDC 6600 of 1964 used a 'scoreboard' to let instructions overlap. Robert Tomasulo designed a scheme for running floating-point instructions out of order in IBM's System/360 Model 91, delivered in 1967. Intel's Pentium Pro of November 1995 brought full out-of-order execution to PC processors, translating x86 instructions into simple internal operations. The MIPS R10000 of 1996 and the Alpha 21264 of 1998 followed. The Pentium Pro's P6 design returned in Intel's Pentium M in 2003.`,facts:[`The Pentium Pro of 1995 could keep about 40 instructions in flight.`,`Tomasulo published his scheme for the Model 91 in 1967.`]},"tech:gfxaccel":{intro:`A 3D graphics accelerator is a chip that does nothing but draw: it fills in triangles with textures, shading and depth, millions of times a second, to build three-dimensional scenes for games. The main processor works out where the triangles are; the accelerator does the heavy pixel work, which would swamp a general-purpose processor. It fits on an add-in card for an ordinary PC.`,how:`Each triangle arrives as three corner points. The chip finds every pixel inside it, looks up each one's colour in a texture image stored in the card's own fast memory, blends neighbouring texture pixels to avoid blockiness, and checks a depth buffer so that nearer surfaces hide farther ones. Dedicated circuits do these steps in parallel pipelines, with no programming beyond setting modes. Software libraries such as OpenGL, Direct3D or a maker's own Glide pass the triangles in.`,history:`Silicon Graphics, founded by Jim Clark in 1982, built costly workstations with 3D graphics hardware used for film effects and flight simulators. Three former Silicon Graphics engineers founded 3dfx Interactive in 1994, and its Voodoo Graphics chipset reached PC add-in cards in late 1996. Games such as Quake, which gained a 3D-accelerated version called GLQuake in January 1997, made the cards popular. NVIDIA, founded in 1993, released its RIVA 128 in 1997 and RIVA TNT in 1998.`,facts:[`Voodoo Graphics cards of 1996 came with 4 MB of memory.`,`The Voodoo chipset of 1996 filled about 50 million pixels a second.`]},"tech:simd":{intro:`SIMD, single instruction multiple data, lets one processor instruction work on several numbers at once. A wide register is split into lanes, such as eight bytes or four decimal numbers, and an addition or multiplication acts on every lane together. Graphics, video, sound and 3D maths repeat the same operation over long rows of data, so they can run several times faster.`,how:`Packed instructions treat a 64-bit or 128-bit register as a row of smaller numbers. They can add with 'saturation', so a bright pixel stops at white instead of wrapping round to black, and they can shuffle and compare lanes. Programs must be rewritten, or compilers improved, to use the new instructions, and data has to be arranged in neat rows. Some versions handle only whole numbers; others handle floating point for 3D geometry.`,history:`Supercomputers such as the Cray-1 of 1976 had used vector instructions on long rows of numbers. Sun added its VIS instructions to the UltraSPARC in 1995, and Intel's Pentium MMX of January 1997 brought 57 packed whole-number instructions to PCs. AMD's 3DNow! of 1998 added floating-point SIMD for 3D games. Intel's SSE, with eight new 128-bit registers, arrived in the Pentium III in February 1999, and Motorola's AltiVec in Apple's Power Mac G4 in August 1999. Intel's AVX of 2011 widened the registers to 256 bits.`,facts:[`MMX of 1997 added 57 instructions working on 64-bit registers.`,`SSE of 1999 could add four single-precision numbers in one instruction.`]},"tech:tnlgpu":{intro:`A GPU with transform and lighting takes over the geometry of 3D graphics as well as the drawing. Besides filling triangles with pixels, the chip now rotates, moves and projects every corner point into view, and works out how the lights shine on each, jobs that kept the main processor busy. The graphics card becomes a processor in its own right.`,how:`Each vertex of a 3D model is multiplied by grids of four by four numbers, called matrices, to place it in the scene and project it onto the screen, then lit by working out its angle to each light. These floating-point sums are repeated millions of times a second. Fixed circuits on the chip do them in parallel, fed by programs through Direct3D or OpenGL. Scenes can carry far more detail without slowing the main processor.`,history:`Professional workstations from Silicon Graphics and others had done 3D geometry in hardware since the 1980s. NVIDIA launched the GeForce 256 in October 1999, marketing it as the world's first 'GPU'. Microsoft's DirectX 7, released in September 1999, supported hardware transform and lighting in games. ATI's Radeon of 2000 added its own transform and lighting engine, and 3dfx, which had none, sold its assets to NVIDIA in December 2000. Microsoft's Xbox of 2001 used an NVIDIA graphics chip.`,facts:[`NVIDIA defined a GPU in 1999 as a chip processing at least 10 million polygons a second.`,`The GeForce 256 of 1999 had four pixel pipelines.`]},"tech:shaders":{intro:`Programmable shaders let game developers write small programs that run on the graphics chip for every vertex or every pixel, instead of choosing from a fixed menu of effects. Water ripples, skin, metal and fog can look exactly as artists want. The graphics processor becomes a programmable parallel computer, running the same short program on thousands of pixels at once.`,how:`A vertex shader moves and animates each corner point; a pixel shader works out the final colour of each pixel from textures, lights and maths. Shader programs are short, from a dozen to about a hundred instructions, with limited precision and no loops. Programs are written in a short, assembly-like code and loaded by the graphics driver. Because each pixel is independent of the others, the chip runs many copies side by side.`,history:`Pixar's RenderMan shading language of 1988 had let film artists program the look of surfaces in software. NVIDIA's GeForce 3, announced in February 2001 for Microsoft's DirectX 8, brought programmable vertex and pixel shaders to consumers. The original Xbox of November 2001 used a GeForce 3-class chip. ATI's Radeon 9700 of 2002 supported DirectX 9 with floating-point pixel shaders. High-level shading languages, NVIDIA's Cg and Microsoft's HLSL, arrived in 2002. NVIDIA released CUDA in 2007, letting programmers use the graphics chip for general calculations.`,facts:[`The GeForce 3 of 2001 had about 57 million transistors.`,`DirectX 8 vertex shaders of 2001 allowed up to 128 instructions.`]},"tech:phonesoc":{intro:`A phone system-on-chip puts nearly all the electronics of a mobile phone onto a single chip: a processor core for applications, a digital signal processor for radio and sound, graphics and video engines, and controllers for the camera, display and memory. Fewer chips mean a smaller, cheaper handset with longer battery life, and the processor can run full applications as well as calls.`,how:`Blocks licensed from different designers, such as ARM processor cores and graphics units, are joined on one die by on-chip buses. Each block can be switched off when it is not needed, and clocks and voltages are tightly managed, because the whole chip must live on a fraction of a watt. Memory chips are often stacked directly on top in the same package. The radio modem may sit on the same chip or on a companion one.`,history:`Texas Instruments' OMAP chips, pairing an ARM core with its own DSP, went into smartphones in the early 2000s, among them many Nokia models. Qualcomm's MSM chips, which had begun as modems for its CDMA phones in the 1990s, added ARM application processors and multimedia engines in the early 2000s. Apple's first iPhone of June 2007 used a Samsung-made chip with an ARM11 core. Qualcomm's first Snapdragon chip, running at 1 GHz, shipped in 2008. Apple's own A4, designed after it bought PA Semi in 2008, appeared in the iPad and the iPhone 4 in 2010.`,facts:[`The first iPhone of 2007 ran its ARM11 processor at about 412 MHz.`,`Apple bought the chip designer PA Semi for about $278 million in 2008.`]},"tech:powermgmt":{intro:`Dynamic power management lets a processor change its speed and voltage from moment to moment to match the work in hand. Running flat out only when needed, and dropping to a crawl at a lower voltage the rest of the time, cuts power use sharply. In a laptop that means hours more battery life and a cooler, quieter machine; in a server, a smaller electricity bill.`,how:`A chip's switching power rises with its clock frequency and with the square of its supply voltage, and a lower clock allows a lower voltage, so slowing down saves power twice over. The operating system watches how busy the processor is and asks for a new 'performance state'; the chip changes its clock multiplier and tells the voltage regulator to step up or down within microseconds. Idle parts of the chip have their clocks stopped, and deeper sleep states cut power to whole sections.`,history:`The ACPI standard of 1996 gave operating systems a common way to manage power in PCs. Intel's SpeedStep, introduced in mobile Pentium III processors in January 2000, switched between two speeds depending on whether a laptop ran on battery or mains. Transmeta's Crusoe, announced the same month in January 2000, adjusted voltage and speed continuously with its LongRun technology. AMD's PowerNow! of 2000 and Cool'n'Quiet of 2003 did the same for its chips. Intel's Pentium M, sold as part of its Centrino platform from March 2003, combined many speed steps with a design built for efficiency.`,facts:[`Halving the supply voltage cuts switching power to about a quarter (2003).`,`The 1.6 GHz Pentium M of 2003 was rated at about 24.5 watts.`]},"tech:x8664":{intro:`Sixty-four-bit computing widens a processor's registers and addresses to 64 bits, so a single program can use far more than the 4 gigabytes that 32-bit addresses can reach. Large databases, servers and scientific programs can keep huge amounts of data in memory at once. The x86-64 design does this while running existing 32-bit software at full speed.`,how:`A 32-bit address can name about four billion bytes; a 64-bit one about 18 billion billion, far more than any machine can hold, so the chips use only 40 or 48 of the address bits. AMD's extension of x86 doubles the general registers from eight to sixteen, each 64 bits wide, which speeds up ordinary programs too. The chip switches between 64-bit mode and the old modes, so a 64-bit operating system can run old applications unchanged.`,history:`Workstation makers had 64-bit processors well before PCs, such as the MIPS R4000 of 1991 and DEC's Alpha of 1992. Intel's Itanium, a new 64-bit design incompatible with x86, launched in 2001 and sold poorly. AMD announced its 64-bit extension of x86 in 1999 and shipped it in the Opteron server chip in April 2003 and the Athlon 64 that September. Intel adopted the same instructions in 2004, and Microsoft released Windows XP Professional x64 Edition in April 2005.`,facts:[`A 32-bit address reaches 4,294,967,296 bytes, about 4 GB (2003).`,`The first x86-64 chips of 2003 used 40-bit physical addresses, up to 1 TB.`]},"tech:multicore":{intro:`A multicore processor puts two or more complete processor cores on a single chip, sharing its link to memory and often a large cache. When one core cannot be made faster without overheating, adding cores gives more total computing power within the same heat limit. Programs must be split into parallel threads to use them fully.`,how:`Each core has its own registers, execution units and small caches, while a shared cache and memory controller tie them together. Hardware keeps every core's copy of memory consistent, so when one core writes a value the others see it. The operating system runs different programs or threads on different cores at once. Two cores at a lower clock can do more work than one fast core using the same power, because power rises steeply with clock speed and voltage.`,history:`IBM's POWER4 of 2001 was the first commercial processor with two cores on one chip, built for servers. Intel cancelled its single-core Tejas design in May 2004, as rising power made higher clocks impractical. Intel's Pentium D and AMD's Athlon 64 X2, the first dual-core chips for PCs, arrived in May 2005. The Xbox 360 of November 2005 had a three-core processor. Intel's first four-core desktop chip, the Core 2 Extreme QX6700, followed in November 2006.`,facts:[`IBM's POWER4 of 2001 had about 174 million transistors.`,`Intel's fastest Pentium 4 of 2004 ran at 3.8 GHz.`]}};export{e as PEDIA_HARDWARE};